Recognizable prostate cancer: to undergo an examination or not?
The PSA test sometimes yielded erroneous results that were incorrectly negative or even improper-positive, which led to unnecessary medical procedures.
All this increased the hesitancy of men, who were already reluctant to go for an examination.
To be examined or not?
We've asked two experts for an opinion.
Studies carried out in the U.S. have experienced a high level of contamination between individual groups tested, so it is difficult to interpret these outcomes and bring valuable recommendations.
Another study, this time European, noted a different mortality rate among patients who underwent an examination and those who did not go to them.
This study has also shown after a 12-year interval that those who don't go for the examination have a 30-to-sufficient risk of metastases.
Therefore, I recommend an examination from 50 years of age or from the age of 40 if your direct relative had prostate cancer.
Men of Afro-American descent are also a more endangered group.
The key is to take the right decision as soon as cancer is revealed.
There are aggressive types of cancer and species that are impertinent.
Therefore, it is necessary to properly explain to the patient the degree of danger of his type of cancer, to offer him possible alternatives, do not necessarily cure prostate cancer, which does not have an impact on life in the long run, and in these cases to focus rather on the active supervision of the disease.
Today, a number of men who have been diagnosed with cancer are untreasoned because their type of cancer is not aggressive and does not endanger their lives.
Rather, we will propose them the implementation of active oversight and, in the case of progression of the disease, we will offer them treatment.
Increasingly increasingly and with greater accuracy, we set criteria that help in decision-making, which species to treat and which does not.
Nevertheless, I recommend undergoing an examination.
However, it is advisable to undergo an interview with a doctor and determine whether it is appropriate to undergo the examination or not.
Collaborating with the International Urological Society, the Movember movement has created a tool that allows it to evaluate pros and cons in connection with PSA examination.
You can substrite this document (at the moment in English, the translation will be available later) at: http://ca.movember.com/fr/mens-health/prostate-cancer
Prevaccination of the disease
Unfortunately, there is no miraculous procedure to prevent cancer.
Contributed to advances in the research, it seems the best way to curb the risks of the disease to adopt the principles of a healthy lifestyle.
Estimates that if everyone accelerating healthily and moving adequately, up to 30% of cancer cases could be avoided.
"If everyone stopped smoking, this figure would rise to at least 50%," says André Beaulieu, a spokeswoman for the Canadian Cancer Research Society.
On the other hand, it is estimated that approximately 10% of the cancer disease causes heredity.
A percentage remains completely unexplactured.
For the canadian cancer research society, the fight against smoking remains a priority, even when the number of smokers is declining.
Cigarettes account for 85% of cases of lung cancer.
They are also a risk factor for several others.
This is extremely detrimental to human health.
"There are still 1.5 million smokers in Quebec today," spokesman André Beaulieu shakes his head.
Encouraging figure: 10 years after the smoker stops smoking, the risk of dying from cancer decreases by half.
Weigh
Bodiness and Obesity, according to SCC, are another risk factor for the occurrence of the disease.
They increase the risk of cancer of the breast, colon and rectum, esophagus, pancreas and uterus.
"The research shows that regular bodily activity throughout its life protects against colon cancer," the report adds.
Eating
This organization also recommends limiting the consumption of red meat.
With too high consumption it can lead to an increased risk of collectal cancer.
It is also advisable to avoid sausages, for which the same applies.
Preservation of meat by smoking, drying or salin can lead to the formation of carcinogenic substances.
"They can inflict damage to the cells of the organism and lead to the development of cancer," the report adds.
Vitamines
Lately, various scientists have focused on the connection between vitamin supplements and cancer.
However, the research has not yet come to unequivocal conclusions.
Studies focused on vitamin E are contradictory according to SCC.
According to one of them, there will be a reduction in the risk of prostate cancer, another study speaks more of an increase.
Even the effect of vitamin D on cancer is unequivocally proven.
Mr. Beaulieu further insists on how important it is to talk about his fears and precedents from the family with his doctor.
"Subsecuting an examination doesn't mean you're going to have cancer."
Higgs Boson Explored
Announcing the probable discovery of the Higgs boson sparked an afflict last summer.
There is an opinion that this boson participates in the mechanism that is responsible for all matter in the universe.
Furthermore, it is the last particle whose existence is foreboded by a standard model - our best or "least wrong" explanation of the nature and behavior of matter - which, however, has not yet been empirically observed.
Among the physicists, however, there is no one-hundred-per-per-per-per-percent certainty that it's Higgs boson.
Undoubtedly, we have a completely new particle at our disposal, which closely resembles the Higgs boson, as it is predicted by a standard model.
The new data published this week during the Great Gyketo Physical Congress rather confirm it, but there is still a lack of all the data so that we can have absolute certainty.
However, let's assume it's the Higgs boson, because the risks of error appear small, and let's take a glimpse of what's really going on.
There is an undisputed law in this world, according to which two things cannot occur at the same point at the same time.
You can’t circumnavigate this rule – and don’t try too diligently, otherwise you’ll hurt yourself.
Although particle physicist is a very strange world, it turns out that a similar law also applies: Pauli's excretory principle, according to which two particles cannot occupy the same space at the same time if they are in the same "quantum state" - this "controuity" roughly creates some of their properties.
Furthermore, physicist particles are divided into two categories.
On the one hand, we will find well-ordered citizens, or fermions that obediently obliously adhere to Pauli's principle.
And at the other end there are bosons, subservient anarchists who do not appreciate anything at all - certainly not this principle, which means that they can easily be in the same place at the same time.
These bosons are further divided into two groups according to the device (by the way absolutely amazing), which is The Particle Adventure, in Berkeley's laboratories: mesons that we leave unnoticed here, and "strength particles", through which great natural forces prodivide, with which the Higgs boson is probably related in some way.
It must be said, however, that these bosons are not as exotic animals as can be imagined.
If you read this article, it is thanks to a boson with an unusuable banality: a photon, or "particle of light", which is an "bearer" of electromagnetic force.
In fact, when a particle with an electrical charge accelerates or changes its direction, it "disturs" the electromagnetic field in this particular place, similar to a pebble thrown into a pond.
This "disturbance" creates an electromagic wave (light, infrared or ultraviolet rays, etc.), and this wave is nothing more than a photon - and thus one of the bosons, "carriers of force".
Stimulating field
It's also with the Higgs bosson, with the only difference from "disturbing" another field, the Higgs field, in order for this boson to appear.
This Higgs field is much, much more stable than an electromagnetic field; its excitation must be attained by very high energetic values, similarly to the frozen surface of the pond, only a very large boulder rips.
For this reason, it is necessary to use such a huge accelerator accelerator as an accelerator in the CERN laboratory - a large Hadron accelerator with accommodating beam is a circle with a circumference of 27 km - in order to exert such energy.
An analogy with an electromagic field fits us again to elucidate the relationship between Higgs and matter.
With the electromagnetic field, neither all the particles nor all the materials are responded.
Some, for example, magnets, do so, but others don't - a piece of paper, for example, it will never be held by itself on the refrigerator.
Likewise it is true that all particles do not react with the Higgs field: those that do so have a certain mass, while others (such as photon, let’s look at it) don’t.
And Mrs. Nováková asks what all these researches are for, what do they "bring to us"?
From the point of view in terms of science, it is a confirmation of the validity of the standard model (SM), which allows physicists to investigate any inconsistency between observations and predictions of SM.
Moreover, many physicists literally want them to be found, because the slightest difference could open the space of "new physicists" and seal some gaps in the model.
It is necessary to state that the model still has huge deficiencies, brings no clarification of gravity (deep!) or dark matter, which accounts for approximately 80% of the cosmic matter (again).
CERN equipment, however, has not yet emerged such discrepancies.
Responsibility
Responsibility of this research in the daily lives of Mr and Ms Novák is very difficult to predict, but it would be a mistake to think that there will be none.
Look: in the very early 60's, laser pioneers in Bell's laboratories didn't even have an inconsumption of a revolution that their work will evoke.
They anticipated scientific use, but nothing else.
Physician Willard Boyle - who worked at the Bell Labs, where the laser was invented in 1960, and who created the first permanent laser beam (previous ray rays were pulsable) in 1962 I have already said this - the laser was initially regarded as such a "lagent toy".
Just imagine it...
In addition, the application can also result from all the tools that surround the research.
For example, the same Willard Boyle created a small light-a-sensor in 1969 during his work focused on optics.
This sensor, although Boyle didn't plan it at first at all, now serves as an "eye" of all digital cameras in the world, earning him the Nobel physici-physicist in 2009.
Of course, it doesn’t mean that activities around LHC necessarily have to change our lives, but it doesn’t really mean that one really never knows.
Palliative care - The best way to get out of life... | Le Devoir
Through his Dignified Eager Commission, Quebek has recently been involved in a delicate end-of-life question.
The debate is expected to continue shortly and a bill is being prepared at the same time.
There is still a lot of work to be done.
Le Devoir has attempted to take a closer look at this issue.
Less than a few weeks a few weeks ago, Mr. L. was still living alone at his apartment in Montenegrin.
The prostate guineafest that acquainted him provided him with a two-year delay.
"They've given me five years of my life, and it's already seven," he says without emotion on his bed in a palliative-paying house Victor-Gadbois in Beloeil, where the previous day had arrived.
"But it's still a shock, you can't prepare for this," he adds.
Illness has done her work: an immense weakness prevents him from going to the toilet himself or eating alone.
Resulled, he watches a tasty-looking breakfast and agrees that we will help him to eat.
He is courageous and he even smiles, he talks to strangers who stop at him, bring him medicine, offer a bath.
Courage for an ordinary death.
"My biggest wish is to get rid of this diarrhea, it humiliates me," he confides.
A few hours later a team of doctors found a cure for his problem.
"In all his life, a person learns that men urinate on a standstill," rehearses Pierre Brodeur, a psychologist at Victor-Gadbois.
Recurregnising to a child is an unacceptable humiliation for some people.
“It depends on the abilities of each person,” the psychologist said.
According to a number of people engaged in palliative care, crucial moments take place at the very heart of such a return.
Patients embarking on a palliative-distant Victor-Gadbois home are all suffering from cancer.
Their prognosis is a maximum of three months of life.
At this stage, a team of doctors and nurses who take care of them does not provide so-called "treatment" care.
In the case of Mrs. A., 89, the worst fear is to die "on suffocation in full consciousness."
Illness has allowed me to discover my children.
"I've got good kids," he adds.
"I don't have any more desires in my life," he says, and then he agrees to put on her breathing apparatus.
Nevertheless, he is still looking forward in the following days to the last visit of his son from Italy.
At Victor-Gadbois, in cooperation with a group of volunteers, provides body care with the help of nourishment.
This is what palliative care looks like if nothing else can no longer be done.
To make death more comfortable.
Québek has one palliative palliative care bed for 11,700 inhabitants.
That's very little if we realize we're all going to die one day.
Here, life continues in optimal conditions, Dr. Christiane Martel, one of the doctors in the home, explains.
Whether it's a level of physical, emotional or spiritual comfort.
When a person dies, it is possible to provide, for example, a sip of brandy or pepsi, according to their taste.
Diabetics don’t have to keep an eye on sugar.
Death is part of everyday life.
Last night they bore a beer to Mr. X, who later died last night.
This morning his son finishes beer in honor of the deceased father.
"We're assisting patients just like their loved ones," Nathalie Savard, director of the nursing care service, recounts.
In Victor-Gadbois's home, the days go one after another, but they don't resemble each other.
In addition to the ninety-three-year-old-year-old, who rejoices in the last meeting with his family thoroughly laid out on pillows while tossing him tossing him, a tragic thirty-six-year-old man dies, surrounded by his parents, a wife and two young children after trying all the chances of surviving.
"For half a year there have always been three to five beds, which are designed for people with cancer up to the age of 45 years old," says Christiane Martel.
53% of patients who come into the Victor-Gadbois home come from their homes, 47% from hospitals.
Difficulties with access to palliative care
It is rummagined that 77% of Canadians simply don't have access to palliative care, i.e. the care that's designed to alleviate suffering as soon as the patient arrives at the final stage of his life, whether it's at home, in a hospital or in a nursing home.
And several organizations, such as Victor-Gadbois home and Grand Montréal Home palliative Treatment Society, specialize more or less exclusively in the care provided to onkological patients.
And it is this fundamental unavailability of health care in Quebec that a number of doctors specializing in palliative care are worried about the adoption of the Euthanasia Act and Assisted Suicide.
As early as early as Ocuthancio, there has been a manifesto signed by leaders in the field of palliative treatment, such as Dr. Balfour Mount and Dr Bernard Lapointe, demonstrating their disagreement with such an initiative.
According to Doctor Christiane Martele, Quebec's health care system isn't powerful enough to provide access to high-quality palliative care before euthanasia can only be agreed to perform euthanasia.
As she says, she has recently witnessed a patient who has spent a 14-hour emergency room in huge pain, without anyone taking the necessary steps to relieve her.
They are afraid that patients should not ask for death because they are not receiving appropriate care.
At the same time, many oncologists take care of their patients until the last moments, regardless of an unfavorable prognosis.
Hélène Richard's chances of survivation have already been minimal when she had abandoned demanding chemotherapy.
Responding when I communicated to my encoding that I was quitting treatment, she replied that she regretted losing my fight, telling us.
And yet she told me it was over!
There is no all-powerful care
Dr. Martel estimates that 90% of patients who wish to die thank their attending physicians for not fulfilling their wishes when the palliative cuddling team eased their pain.
However, it must be said that paliative care is definitely not omnipotent in the treatment of pain.
According to Elsie Monereau, Palliative Care Director at the Grand Montréal Home Palliative Care Society, patients are defending the treatment of pain in 8% of cases.
At the very end of his life, doctors often use palliative sedatives, which will put the patient to sleep until the moment of death, whether in a shock or whether the sedatives are administered permanently.
It is not possible to pretend that we do not perceive this part of suffering.
A patient we can't get rid of the pain will have an increasing opportunity to access palliative sedatives.
Slurs who are not given sedatives always say the same: “I want to die”.
But it doesn’t always necessarily mean “I want you to give me euthanasia,” it means “I want you to get rid of my pain.”
