Thanks to Bitcoin you can pay and sell without banks
All the negatives of current financial transactions based on central banks, identified transactions and processing costs between recipients.
Moreover, as is often the truth within these technologies, there is an apparent political prospect: the belief that the existing financial system built on banking monopolies leads to financial crises.
Bitcoin, whose inventor is Satoshi Nakamoto ( pseudonym), represents both virtual money (transparable to dollars, euros...) and a secure BitTorrent-like transfer protocol, which allows you to exchange files between pairs of partners.
Thanks to 15 000 computers plugged into the network, about 200 000 transactions have already been registered.
About a thousand websites already adopt bitcoins as gifts or as a means of payment.
The exchange rate of bitcoin first reached a high of $30 ($23) in June 2011, fell to $2 five months later, today it is at the limit of about ten dollars (excrete overview of odds is listed on bitcoincharts.com).
Compared to the world in exchangation for real money or financial products, it is nothing impressive.
Nevertheless, the European Central Bank (ECB) is concerned about this in its virtual finance report, which came out in October.
Bitcoin refers to "the most successful virtual money", "competing the dollar and the euro" and the "similes of ordinary money".
Bitcoin differs from other types of virtual money in that it is as "credits" used to advance in a video game that a person gains with a game or that you can buy (and sometimes re-repeat).
The social network Facebook also developed a similar system.
Every month, however, the central authority inspects and processes individual communications.
In the case of Bitcoin, all nodes of the network are depositaries of current account books, controllers, issuers of money and buyers and sellers as well.
How does this network work?
Each transaction between two users actually takes place between two e-mail addresses.
However, the user can choose a different address for each payment, ensuring an anonymity.
A set of information related to this Transperiurtle is electronically double-creed encryption system.
The network can thus verify the legitimacy of the transaction.
Thanks to the content of the file, it is also possible to ensure that the exchanged bitcoins exist in the public book of current accounts, which is distributed all over the network.
The key phase consists in the entry of this new transaction into this book.
It proceeds by resoluring the mathematical task assigned to computers, the solver of which, a temporary central banker, will have the right to write this additional line.
It's a file cut-out phase, i.e. the transformation of a huge file into a shorter and unique digital imprint.
Computers will "take over" this new transaction and add a number to it, only then "skewer the whole".
The goal is to find a number that provides a specific imprint (a lot zero at the beginning).
As soon as this number is found, other nodules easily check its correctness.
Transaction is then indevitably displayed indefinitely connected to the string of all other transactions; any modification would change the imprint.
If the user would like to pay twice with the same money very quickly (within ten minutes), the network confirms only one of the two transactions - the other will be abandoned, because each of them has a different imprint.
A computer that solves the challenge wins 50 bitcoins.
In order to avoid inflation, this reward is regularly divided into two, probably until the end of 2012.
The number of bitcoins in circulation is thus consequent 21 million, but they can be divised up to a hundred-millionth, so there is...
Difficulty of the assigned task also increases with an increasing calculation level.
The network has already experienced its ascension and falls.
Websites that provide services for Bitcoin have been hacked and stored bitcoins have been stolen.
“Userated loophole does not apply to the protocol itself,” says Pierre Noizatat, who founded Paymium, a real-bearing-dollar-dollar-dollar-dollar-grown-dollar-grown-dollar-grown-dollar company.
The ECB also monitors money laundering options thanks to this anonymous service.
But this problem also applies to cash.
Significant players such as Wikipedia deny the donations of this nature.
Others, as a platform of WordPress blogs, are accepting them.
Adi Shamir and Dorit Ron of the Weizmann institute in Israel recently analyzed a book of current bills and proved that almost 80% of bitcoins do not circulate.
“Huge repetitions” were launched in November.
"Thirty thousand dollars have been exchanged," says Jon Holmquist, who works for Coinabul dealing with converting bitcoins into gold.
Pierre NoIzat, who is also the author of the pedagogical book regarding this money, has great confidence in the potential of this technology as a transaction network.
Its system, Paytunia, is equivalent to a credit card (with actual money) or contactless mobile payments, but it uses a Bitcoin system to authenticate transactions that make it cheaper.
In addition, the wearer manages his/her identity and may remain anonymous.
The system can be easily adjusted for merchants where you don't need to install new terminals or software.
It is enough to provide an address that can be "photographed and distinguished on the phone", clarified by Pierre Noizat, which assures that it already has thousands of users.
There is a general movement into questioning of hierarchical systems and the transition to more horizontal systems.
“It’s going to take a long time before Bitcoin succeeds, it’s going to take a long time,” he said.
Moreover, the ECB envisages re-evaluation of the various risks, which are considered to be increased today, as long as this kind of money is successful.
