About 65% of the population is under the age of 25; there are currently 5 million inhabitants in Kabul, one-fifth of the total population.
In the cities there are state schools full, girls and boys go there.
These young people will have to be given work, they no longer want to go back to obstetrics of earlier parties or to the corruption of some representatives.
Everyone, including an armed opposition, enjoys mobile phones; television and Turkish series that show the modern world are watched everywhere.
The army is already present.
Will the authorities put themselves at its forefront be considered legitimate?
Former commander-in-chiefs against the Soviet army are already resuming provincial militias, which will be outside the control of central power.
Afghanistan, a mountainous country with a strong influence of local identities, should look forward to a degree of decentralization following the example of Western states, the U.S. wanted to transform it into a centralized state with a strong presidential influence, so there was an abolition of the position of the prime minister, which has existed since the adoption of the constitution in 1964.
President Karzai does not want foreign controls, especially on the occasion of elections scheduled for April 2014.
However, his country has been significantly dependent on foreign aid since the 50's and previous years.
No industry has been reconstructed, no dam is functioning, no major irrigation system has been repaired.
