Without the energy for the processing of ferrous ore on the site and without means of transporting raw materials through the mountains, however, no mineral extraction can take place.
The Chinese have already almost left the Mes Aynak copper mine, leaving international archaeological teams (with the support of the World Bank) the opportunity to explore a sprawling Buddhist site to preserve the role of the most important employer across the province.
On a single day it will also be necessary to rebuild normal ties between Afghanistan and Pakistan, on which a large portion of imports and exports.
Departure of the French combat units was completed on November 20.
The new collaborative co-operation presupposes the continuation of common types of assistance: girls' and boy lyceca, departments of French at Universities, French institute, collaborating in the military, legal, medical and agricultural fields, support for an archaeological delegation.
Since 2009, an "acquisition of the heart and soul" initiative has been operational, attempting a diligent task of reconciliation of assistance and military activities, initiating the "civil-employment steps" of the Ministry of Defense (Cimic), which was abolished in 2012, but, thanks to a small French NGO, achieved a number of achievements in the area of mutual interest and steps for agricultural restoration in dozens of mountain villages.
These works, in which a number of local workers were involved, helped to dampen the uprising: irrigation, wells, drinking water, archinations, fruit trees, soil protection and the expansion of a cultivated area.
What remembrance will we leave after two billion euros of military spending?
A much more modest budget would contribute to the improvement of local living conditions, which are very demanding in these valleys, which are often located at an altitude of more than 2,000 m.
The embassy received dozens of written requests for petty agricultural assistance from local communities in the province of Kapisa.
In order to liberate themselves from the upbringings led by foreign groups, these farmers wish to achieve shrewd civic assistance on their behalf, which would be duly inspected and touch them directly.
Difficult work on the Egyptian Constitution
President Mohammed Morsi has embarked on another game of poker.
While Egypt stays most divided in history due to the proclamation of a constitution temporarily confiscating unrestrained powers, he decided to play vabank.
Speasurrhed on Wednesday, notwithstanding everyone else's opinion, the Constituent Assemblies will approve the final wording.
Less than a week less than a week ago, the head of state awarded the assembly another two months to complete the necessary work.
For nearly two years, Egypt has used a makeshift text that has been supplemented many times, which weakens the constitutional stability of the country and leads to legal conjecture.
This new initiative only further strengthened the ambivalence of the country.
From the point of-of-o-issuing opposition, the president persists in his "autocratic delirium", he continues to "shake his word" and "tramples on the law."
His supporters, however, reassure that it is a means of curbing with a constitutional and political crisis as quickly as possible and to accelerate the transitional process.
A resumption should take place in two weeks.
It is a very short period of time, which will force the Brotherhood to resuperate the text to the Egyptians to clarify the article after the article.
For the president, it is also a way to regain national and democratic legitimacy, while the whole country is torn by protests.
Concerned in an interview with U.S. Weekly Time, Mohammed Morsi appears to be convinced that the Egyptians will vote in favour.
Particularly because this hasty-occupied poll puts the Egyptian people in front of an ultimatum: "Either you will approve my text, or I will keep unlimited powers," which should otherwise end after the approval of the new constitution.
And in this special atmosphere, 85 deputies of the aforementioned Constituent Assemblance with a large majority of Islamists approved the text yesterday.
The majority of liberals were absent in the vote.
In mid-Nomber, not long before the constitution was pronounced, they banished the door, saying that they do not have the opportunity to assert their views.
Advocators of human rights, civil society or members of religious minorities imitated their move.
In order to quorate the quoruity, 11 substitutes were philanthroquiously republished yesterday morning.
Some of them are very oblivious to the Muslim fragrant.
Unsurprisingly, the articles of the constitution have often been voted unanimously.
Commentators further questioned the fact that one of the nurculous controversies revolved around that day... an hour for prayer, when some members of the committee held the opinion that the lessons in the meeting room were not properly adjusted.
The text, which was filed just before last night, consists of 234 articles.
The subject-sanctuary of General Attemporal, Article 2, ultimately reciproconceds as the version of the Constitution of 1971 and stipulates that "Sharia's predominance is the main source of law".
Salafist parties, for whom the introduction of Islamic law is one of the fundamental requirements, had hoped to replace the term "principles" with the term "rules", which would allow for stricter application.
For the Islamists the fact that this article has not been corrected, it represents an indication of their goodwill and respect for other parts of the Egyptian society.
“Phypocrismit” is argued by the Liberals, who only see a rhetorical problem.
According to them, the Islamization of the constitution takes place through other articles.
They focus in particular on Article 220 that confers an advisory role at al-Azhar University, in particular for the control of compliance with sharia laws.
Sophie Pommier, an expert on Egypt, this is disturbing because "persons called for expression are unelected and have no democratic legitimacy.
This can be seen as the beginning of theocracy".
The fears of the Liberals are angered by the fact that the next rector of the university should be less moderated than the current one.
"For the time being, there hasn't been a specific responsibility.
With this constitution, Egypt relinquish remains a civil state.
The majority of the lawyers who worked on this text are not lumber, but university workers, many of whom have graduated in French schools," says Alexis Blouet, who prepares work on the constitutional transition in Egypt.
However, it agrees that "There may be contradictions regarding the interpretation of Article 220, because the terms used in it belong to the religious dictionary.
In particular, there is a reference to "fiqh".
And in the future, there may be a question of the extent to what extent civilian judges are competent to comment on this."
In addition to religious sites, the approved text yesterday is highly criticized due to the extended powers it confissed to the President of the Republic.
The Muslim fraternity insists there has been a significant resumption compared to the former regime.
Another contentious place: the powers entrusted to the army.
In accordance with the wishes of the representatives of the mistige, the verification of the defense budget will not be carried out by parliament, but by the National Defense Council.
Furthermore, there will be no banning proceedings against civilians in military courts, as required by the association for the protection of human rights.
In connection with the text, which according to them is directed against freedom, these associations speak about their doubts.
Blasphemy's offense is preserved and an offense continues to be banned, which could have serious consequences for freedom of expression, especially for the press.
In addition, there is an article about the protection of women, according to Heba Morayef of the Human Rights Watch organization.
According to her, the only positive point is the prohibition of torture referred to in Article 36.
This word has not been Mentioned in the previous constitution.
While the Egyptian president was scheduled to perform in a televised speech last night, demonstrations are scheduled for this afternoon.
Supporters of the head of the state will then be demonstrating on the Sabbath.
In Israel awaits Ukrainian tourists holy places, the navel of the world and the seas from saturated salvo solution
The Promised Earth combines the greatness of biblical truths, modern comfort and inconspicuity of nature.
AaF has identified the five most important reasons why you should visit Israel.
Pleniest the Holy Places
To visit the Jordan River where Jesus was baptized.
It is asserted that God’s mercy will come down on all those who enter this “bath.”
And Galilee - instead of the miracles of Jesus: the transformation of water into wine at the wedding, walking on the water, calming the storm and fulfilling the networks.
Here Jesus also appeared to his disciples and after the resurrection.
But most of the holy places are in Jerusalem.
Believers will go through the Path of Pain or the Way of the Crusade of Jesus (Ladinally Via Dolorosa).
It begins at Antonia - Pretorio - where the trial took place, walks through the streets of the Old Town and leads to the Temple of the Holy Sepulchre on the mountain of Golgotha - to the place of crucifixion, the stone of anointing and the place where Jesus was buried.
There is a symbolic Christian navel of the world that symbolizes the sardiness of humanity.
The Monastery of the Holy Cross in Jerusalem was erected in a place where, according to the Christian legend, a tree grew, from which they made a cross for the crucifixion of Jesus.
There is also the hosifiest place for the Jews in Jerusalem - the Wall of Lamentations, the remnant of the temple, which was destroyed by the Romans in 70 AD.
Depending on the tradition, people of different confessions leave notes with wishes, which will then come true.
Take a journey around the vertical
The ruins of Masada's fort remained from a secret hideout from the enemy that Herod the Great had built in 25 BC for his family.
Ruin is located on cliffs in the mountains at an altitude of 450 m above sea level.
Only those engaged in mountaineering can reach it on their own.
The others will be taken by a cable car to this historic mountain.
And in the north of the country at an above-altitude height of 1600-2040 m there is the well-known ski resort Hermon, which is experiencing a huge influx of tourists in the winter months.
To this ski paradise transports tourists from the footsteps of a shuttle bus from the foothills.
The total length of the mountain ski slopes is 45 km.
According to an old-fashioned legend, pagan gods reportedly lived on top of the mountain.
Visit unique museums
There are about 300 museums in this country.
You can't visit them all during one trip.
But at at least five of them, the most interesting, are definitely worth a visit.
Among them is the Museum of Israel, which is located close to Knesset (Parliament).
In it are stored ancient Qumran manuscripts, or comers from the Dead Sea, which have been found in the Caves of the Judean Desert, and approximately 500 thousand archaeological and anthropological exhibits.
It is also worth attending the Tel-A Aviv Museum of Art.
Impressionists and expressionists Monet, Pissarro, Renoir, Cézanne, Sisley, Matisse, Modigliani, Chagall, Picasso are widely represented in its canvas.
In Akkon you can visit the Al Basha Museum-Cazne, which presents visitors several rooms of the ancient Turkish bath with mannequins of spa users and spa caregivers from that time.
In Caesarea it is worth a visit a unique private Ralli museum, where you can admirate the statues of Dalí and Roden.
There are no guides or souvenir shops.
Entrance fee is free and donations are strictly rejected.
The Fifth Museum of the Holocaust or Yad Vashem in Tel Aviv tells about one of the most dramatic aspects of history.
The most tragic part of the museum - Children's Memorandum - was erected to commemorate 1.5 million children who were murdered in concentration camps and gas chambers.
You will enter - and you will find yourself in complete darkness.
The stars shimmer.
And there are the names of Jewish children and the names of the countries in which they have perished.
Ukraine is also mentioned.
Improving your health
Israel's three main spa resorts are located on the coasts of the Mediterranean, the Red and the Dead Sea.
Each of them offers swimming pools, aqua parks, dolphinariums and oceanariums.
Interestingly it is possible to bathe in the Red Sea even in the winter months, because the water temperature does not deciduce below 21 degrees Celsius and the air is heated up to 23 degrees.
The Dead Sea is even holier, and in which people bathe throughout the year.
The Dead Sea, by the way, is the strangest sea in the world, located at the lowest mainland point on the planet - at 417 m below sea level.
Côte d'A Cô was a saturated sausage solution, which easily keeps on the surface even people who can't swim.
Surrounding landscape is unrealistically beautiful.
Embark here to undergo treatment using salt water - brine and medicinal mud, which can treat health problems with dermatitis, allergies, asthma, eczemas, arthrosia, arthromite, bronchitis, diabetes to curl your netted nerves.
Affect the Mysterious Ancient Past
It is protected by an ancient part of Tel Aviv - the town of Jaffa on the coast of the Mediterranean Sea.
The famous nautical route connecting Egypt, Syria, Anatolia and Mesopotamia passed through them.
Mentations of this city can be found in ancient Greek and Egyptian legends.
According to the legend, it is the place where Noah built his ark, and Perseus saved the beautiful Andromeda, with which they then spent a long and happy life together.
Tourists really love wanderings through narrow streets, named after the zodiac signs of the zodiac.
It is said that if you cling yourself to the walls in the aisle of "your" sign, your happiness will be favorably incredulously.
In Jaffa you may meet the newlyweds who come here from all over Israel and even from other countries to be photographed.
And in Caesarea - in King Herod city - you can take a glimpse of the Roman theater and "completion" the fortress of the crusaders.
In Roman times Caesarea was the capital of Judea and the seat of Roman Prosecutors, including Pilate of Pontius.
A carefully reconstructed theatre today is used for evening concerts and opera performances.
Hikers, for a word
If you are going to Israel, you don’t have to worry about your imperfeitable English: in this country, about 30% of the population speaks Russian.
It is better to take money not in euros for the journey, but in dollars, which you can easily exchange for shekels (it's about $3.8 shekel).
Urban transport is mainly buses, but in Jerusalem there is a speed tram and in the city of Haifa there is the only metro line in the country with six stations that connect the lower city with the upper one.
Basically, it's an underground cableway.
It costs 6 shekels to travel through all kinds of urban public transport, for which you can move for 1.5 hours.
In Israel, according to Jewish tradition, Sabbath is celebrated on the Sabbath on Saturday.
From Friday evening to sunbath on the Sabbath ceases to work in marketplaces, in shops and on public transport.
And on Sunday morning the working week begins.
In many cafeterias, restaurants and hotels you only get kosher meals: with the exception of pork, seafood, fish without scales and meals, which contain combinations of milk and meat.
They offer a large selection of dishes from lamb and beefs, soups and desserts, prepared from coconut milk, traditional Jewish porridge hummus, various types of sauces, balls from ground chickpeas - falafels, fruits and vegetables.
There are no stray dogs on the streets of Israeli cities.
On the other hand, there are a lot of fattened cats, which gracefully "float according to their taste".
In the evening you can see them sleeping even on the roofs of parked cars.
Leaning cats love populated places and will gladly embark on every goodness.
Renting a car will cost from $37 a day (Hyundai Getz) to $188 (Audi A6 (Audi A6, Volvo S80) per day.
Plus insuity - $15 per day.
Borrowing a bike will cost around 15 shekels a day.
Submitted to museums costs an average of 30 shekels.
Exemblished by numbers
In 2012, more than three million tourists from all over the world visited Israel.
Recognising the country and rest came the most citizens of the USA, Russia, France, Germany, Italy, England and Ukraine.
From January-October 2012, 118.8-thousand Ukrainian tourists, which is 51% more than in the same period in 2010, pre-Accurreviated Visa Waiver on February 9, 2011.
"Greater and mighty" announces it to Moscow: emigrants will save money on the language
While deputies and protectors of human rights are still debating the law on mandatory language exams, there have already been scavators in the country who sell "forged" certificates
13 million foreign workers come to Moscow, St. St. Petersburg and other Russian cities every year.
Most of them are citizens of Central Asia: Uzbekistan, Tajikistan, Turkmenia.
Their only goal is to earn a lot of money to support families at home.
On December 1, a law came into force, according to which every working migrant will be required to pass an examination in knowledge of the Russian language.
So far so far, this rule applies only to those who intend to work in the services, housing and communal economy, lifestyle and retail sales sectors.
FMS, however, promises that in time the relevant tests will be mandatory for all visitors.
Examinations, however, will consist not only of language proficiencies, but also of Russian history and the basics of law.
The knowledge of the language will also have to be confirmed for obtaining and extending the work permit.
Exceptions apply only to citizens of countries where Russian is an official language.
This law is not subject even to people who have successfully achieved attestation and obtained an academic diploma into the disintegration of the USSR in 1991.
Utilization, Convicted and Protection of Rights
A network of testing venues will work under the auspices of Pushkin's Institute of the Russian Language, Universities of Friendship Between Peoples, Mosquisculating State Universities in St. St. St. St. St. St. St. Petersburg and other Russian Universities.
Migrants can pass exams in all cities; more than 160 such centers are already opened in Russia.
The initiative to support the implementation of the testing was also supported by Members of the State Duma and FMS (Federal Migratory Services).
But before the law came into force, human rights defenders have repeatedly been able to ask in the press the question: and what will it actually bring?
What will change the obligation to know the language for the Russians and for the visitors themselves?
First of all, the representatives of the migratory service comply - this will make it possible to reduce the number of people suffering as a result of "slave labor."
A lot of people today talk about protecting the rights of migratory workers, Viktor Sebelev, Chairman of the Board of Directors of FMS in Tajikistan, explains Viktor Sebelev.
The protection of their rights should be initiated even before their departure.
It will only make it possible to solve the problems of foreign workers from 90%.
Problems arise for emigrants without a profession, without education, who do not know the language and do not have a medical examination.
Without his knowledge of the language - he says with the conviction of Sebelev - the future migrant is doomed into clashes with dishonest people who, under the pretext of help, provide him with a "voucher" into ghastly, tight residential hotels, in which he and dozens of others, such as himself, will suffer for months without food and documents, and work for 12 to 14 hours a day.
We receive a lot of grieving complaints from our migrant workers.
"In their homeland they promise not one thing, but upon arrival they cheat them, confiscate their passports, they don't pay the promised wages," confirms Tolib Sharipov, Head of the Task Force of Labor Migration.
Don't scout!
All the less so that many citizens of the Central Asian republics, who intend to go to Russia for work, recognize that they not only do not know the language of the country they want to get into, but they do not even have the experience of the work.
Of course, this is not so much their fault, but rather misfortune: very few Turkmen, Uzbeks, Tajiks can afford to obtain at least an adequate primary education.
After all, their families also need something to feed the children, not to mention the fact that it is necessary to pluck them decently, put on and equipped to school.
As children grow into the working age, they have to go for work at the first opportunity.
It's hard without knowing the language - they admit.
“You feel humiliated and inferior.”
It is true that, in view of the Language Executions, human rights defenders point to one important thing.
Testing will only be carried out on those migrants who have a legal position.
If they don't have it, they won't either pass the exams or get any more official work.
In the meantime, most migratory workers, as they used in Russia, live illegally.
"Greater, or Unauthorized Entrance Disabilities"
Many incoming workers argue that obtaining an official status in our country isn't easy in any way.
The reason - bureaucratic obstructions and all those linguistic difficulties.
In addition, legalization costs money: from 12 to 16 thousand rubles.
But a false registration can be obtained quickly and only one and a half thousand.
The employees of the Russian police know that we basically have fake documents, without registration, and from this flows cases of extortion.
"Require a Hundred, two for cigarettes, a tea," he shared with reporters a citizen of Tajikistan Umed Khuškadamov.
" Come on, don't be stingy, buy a painting."
On the first day of the language law, it was found that not only migrant registration documents were false.
FMS employees have already confisigrated several false certificates of passing the language exam.
Counterfeit documents are imprinted on an Ordinary color printer.
Of course, they did not get to the new owners for free: each of the migrants, in the hope of facilitating the problem of undergoing tests, paid seven thousand rubles for this document.
That's two-and-a-a-half times the amount higher than passing an official test: it will cost migrant workers three thousand rubles.
The main task of the immediate future is to secure the system against corruption, so that workers cannot simply buy the documents, government clerks and protectors of human rights coincide accordingly.
In the meantime, the governing authorities can promise foreign workers, who have not mastered the tests at first, the necessary time to take a course in the basics of the Russian language.
Visitors who don't speak Russian is expected to work in areas where active dealing with people is not required.
MVD (Ministry of the Interior) doesn't put into circulation an illegal arms market
Proportion of criminal offences using a legally-monitored weapon is very low
MVD of Russia submits a proposal to tighten the law in relation to owners of civilian weapons.
It's the reaction of the government's authorities to recent events: the impliions were cases of shooting at weddings that went without casualties and a massacre prepared by Moscow attorney Dimitry Vinogradov, resulting in seven people perished.
The police want to ban the wearing of dangerous guns in public places and increasing the age from which it is possible to obtain a license to carry weapons - from the age of 18 to 21.
This idea is supported by Irina Jaovaya, the head of the Committee on Security for Security and the Fight Against Corruption, who has vowed that changes to the Weapons Act will be presented to the State Duma in the near future.
Not everyone is satisfied that the Russian state authorities are trying to cope with the problem on the "tightening of screws".
An outspoken letter has emerged in the network whose authors - representatives of various social shooting organizations - are demanding the rejection of a "insignificant tightening" of the law.
Percentage of hooliganism with registered weapons is minimal, Vasily Lesnikov, a lawyer and criminologist in an interview with the Russian BBC news agency, said in an interview.
According to the statistics of the Ministry of the Interior in six months of 2012, 142 crimes were committed for the use of firing weapons registered by the authorities of the Ministry of the Interior, while a total of 1168,000 crimes were committed during the same period.
