Fast-Food and Supersouflage Workers Are Going on Strike in the U.S.
Up to a quarter of all U.S. teenagers will go through work at the box office "McDonald"
In recent days, there has been a wave of protests against low-wage protests at Walmart hypermarkets and popular fast-food outlets, such as "McDonald", "Burger King", "Taco Bell", "Wendy's" and "Kentucky Fried Chicken".
So far, no one can foresee whether this wave will be transformed into ninth wave, or whether it is destined to perish soon.
Trade unions and a number of left-leaning social organizations support this entire share.
In addition to increasing modest salaries received by rank-and-file workers at Walmart and fast-food systems, the aim of protest organizers is to be created in their trade unions.
At present, this economic sector is practically unspoiled by a trade union movement.
46 cents a year ?
The events began last week after Thanksgiving, on the so-called "Black Friday", when an orgy of cheap sell-offs smashed on America, engulfed millions of inhabitants and in some cases was also accompanied by fights.
On that day, some Walmart employees, in which 2.2 million people work around the world, left their workplaces and demonstrated together with trade unions and left-wing activists of multinational retail chain companies, whose goods are intended for people from a lower- and medium-sized class.
Walmart sells everything all over the world from diaphragm to hunting rifles and car batteries to vacuum cleaners, eggs and milk.
Products in its stores are on average 8% up to 27% cheaper than in large supermarkets.
That’s why many low-wdgly employees in Walmart stores only shop at work.
Availability and assortment of goods have made the Walmart chain one of America's largest companies.
Critics say Walmart can afford to sell goods so inexpensively in part because it pays little for its employees.
They also complain of poor working conditions, such as the lack of automatic trucks or hand-held scanners.
Demonstrators about the "Black Friday" demanded an increase in salaries and complained that the cost of health insurance provided by the company increased sharply from $30 to $100 a month.
Typical Walmart worker receives $9.5 an hour, but it doesn't match the amount of expenses, employees say.
Scientists at the University of California at Berkeley show that if Walmart raises an average wage to $12 an hour, it will cost $3.2 billion a year.
On an average of 1.1 per cent more than the company spends on salaries now.
If Walmart shifts the wage increase fully to the shoulders of consumers, each visit to the store will only cost 46 cents more.
In a year, customers spend about $12.39 more than they do today.
Advocators Walmart pointed out that the protest took place only in nine countries and the company should not cause virtually any damage.
"Black Friday" in the company's stores lasted from 8 a.m. in the evening on Thursday until midnight the following day, and during that time Walmart sold about 5000 items of goods per second.
In total, its treasuries carried out nearly 100 million transactions on "Black Friday".
Dan Fogelman's representative in an interview with the left-leaning Huffington Post website that jobs had been abandoned in a total of "less than five" Walmart employees, and that the protest had been "further media sensation" orchestrated by trade unions.
"Unoccupied treason!"
Protests resumed this week in New York, where Walmart's department stores weren't their targets (threats in this progressive city aren't very popular, and that's why they're almost unparalleled), but "McDonald" and other inexpensive restaurants.
McDonald's says it sells billions of servings, and then it doesn't even pay your sick pay and doesn't pay you properly for your honest work!
Jumaane Williams, New York City Counsel
At present, the minimum wage for work in accordance with federal laws and New York state regulations is $7.25 per an hour.
Fast-food restaurants are gradually gaining this wage, but only slightly, and on average, their rank-and-file workers receive $8.$8.90 per annum.
There's no lower wage in this expensive city.
I don’t really understand how you can exist in New York with that kind of money.
Nearly a quarter of U.S. teenage thugs who work part-time after school and live with their parents will be employed by "McDonald".
Fenuse people see this as their main source of sustenance or plan to stay there for a long period of time.
At the moment, I am constantly in an interview with "McDonald" workers who are complaining that they have to live out of this money and sometimes even feed their children from them.
On the other hand, there is a comment on the Wall Street Journal forum, the author noticing it is irresponsible to have children if you don't know how to feed them.
Attendees, which began at 6:30 a.m. on "McDonald" at 40th Street and Madison Avenue, demanded that cashiers and chefs in fast-food establishments to start receiving at least $15 an hour, more than double their current minimum wage.
They also demand that trade unions be established in the fast-food sector.
U.S. bills forbade the administration to defend the establishment of trade unions and punish trade union activists by bullying and lay-off.
On the other hand, administration rarely makes their lives easier.
Introduce trade unions in fast-fashioned enterprises, however, it is also difficult for objective reasons.
One of the main reasons is extraordinarily high employee turnover.
Disagreement
Noisy protests also took place on this day in a number of other low-cliesting restaurants in Manhattan.
The culmination of the event at noon was a rally with "McDonald" near the Times Square, where several local Democratic politicians came out, in particular city council member Jumaane Williams, who said: "McDonald" says he sells billions of portions, and then doesn't even pay you sick and doesn't pay you properly for your honest work!"
Demonstrators have also received support for other prominent New York Democrats, such as the mayor-mayor's mayor's account Bill de Blasio, who said that "we have to come together to support fast-food workers in order to achieve a fair reward for their work and economic prosperity that every New Yorker deserves!"
As the New York Times conceived, it was the biggest action of its kind in the history of the U.S. fast-suspite industry.
But only a few hundred people took part in it, although not all of them were fast-suitive employees who employ tens of thousands of people in New York.
Whether this event becomes a spark that will ignite the flame of the mass-moving movement is still unclear.
"So far the brain has been deceiving on average."
Among the fans of modern virtual reality technologies is a popular the theme of augmented reality; lately, it means to perceive the world especially through the prism of special spectacles.
In the early summer, at its annual conference, Google demonstrated the working model of the novelty and it was announced in November that Microsoft also filed an application for the relevant patent.
An interview with the head of an interactive 3D-technology group at Microsoft's Cambridge lab, Sharamo Izadi, shows that for the company's scientists, goggles are already a thing of the past.
They are attracted by the pro Perspective of manipulations of virtual objects in the air with bare hands, the creation of virtual public spaces.
- Please tell us what your research group is doing?
- We deal with the interactions of a person with machines, but at the same time we try to shift the boundaries of this interaction.
Humanity has so far been stranded at the stage of working with pixels on a flat screen, at most it sometimes touches the screen with its finger.
We want to look for 5-10 years ahead and anticipate significant changes in this interaction.
For example, the Xbox console and Kinect sensors - this is a step forward, and almost no Xbox today is for sale without Kinect sensors, because there is a very interesting gesture control option for everyone.
- What's waiting for us in the future?
- Regardless, Kinect has shifted the interaction to the physical level, very many interactions still take place on a flat screen, sometimes in 3D.
The input flow of information has been improved (the system receives a larger amount of data), but the output flow has not been separately so far.
We are trying to change this, we are working on really three-difficult imaging systems based on a variety of technologies, including projective ones.
It is necessary to let the computer world into our physical world, make it more realistic.
For this purpose, however, it is necessary to get to know not only the user, but also the space around it.
Then we will be able to replenish virtual objects into the real world in a much more suitable form.
And first of all - to the hell with those silly helmets of virtual reality!
- What do you think about voice control?
That's a popular thing, but isn't overestimated?
- Certainly it can not be considered a panacea - the question of privacy arises, because we do not always want to inform others in our surroundings about our activities and intentions.
In fact, all types of interaction with computers are good, but in their own environment.
For example, in order to control devices in public places, we had a project in which we were thinking about gestures, but it wasn't about scourgical, scoundrels.
Gestures wouldn’t be scanned by a device’s camera, but with a bracelet that would follow the movements of the bones and the muscles.
So far it is large, but theoretically it can be reduced to the size of a wristwatch.
In general, however, the future has a mixed type of control, such as a gesture + voice.
- And then how?
- For example, how do you ask me to give you this can of lemonade?
You're going to say it and at the same time you'll show.
Basically, I just talk.
- Oh, it's going to be very difficult to recognize.
- So do you want to get the user to adapt to what a machine can or cannot at the moment?
- It is not necessary at all, but it is a rapprochement.
I think that in the near future we will have to work primarily on the development of new types of sensors, which can more accurately detect human reactions.
For example, it can be laser scoundrels that provide a fairly good in-depth resolution, it is very important.
- If you're supposed to talk about your work with Xbox Kinect sensors, what's your demands for modern cameras?
Isn't there enough resolution, the depth or something else today?
- Basically the current generation - that's what we can rescheduled from in our work on three-dimensional recognition.
Of course, it would be a good idea to acquire 8 megapixels at a rate of 1000 f/s.
But it's not just the megapixels themselves, it's like nuts and diligence.
At the current point of view, all the current technologies are insufficient for us - this adds to the work of the designers of algorithms.
So we have to bear in mind not only the resolution in the Axes of X and Y, but also Z.
So it is also very important the speed, the number of shots per second.
Human movement is quite dynamic, and the current data flows of 30 frames per second are, frankly, a little too little, especially for gestures.
Stephen Betishes from our laboratory in Redmond made a touch sensor with an alloccalauable processing devastation of 1 to 100 ms, with simultaneously mass-produced sensors closer to the second value (60-100 ms).
Not everyone understands how much it affects a person's interaction with a machine.
For me at work, the equivalent of such a device, I just don't need so many touches, but I need some extra shots per second.
- Isn't it necessary to increase the number of cameras?
Thirty "cameras" are now being used in the Kinect system, one of which is actually an infrared transmitter, and the other - receiver-defeated signal.
And already the third camera - it's actually a common visible-band sensor.
It is not used to determine the depth of the object.
A larger number of cameras could potentially solve the problem.
Or aggravate it, because it would increase the necessary volume of calculations carried out.
It would be nice to create a flexible Kinect analog system, tinker with curvature of the location of the cameras and see how it will help in determining a three-dimensional position.
- If I remember correctly, Microsoft (unlike Google) didn't publicly present its glasses.
Don’t you think that when it comes to the daily use of augmented reality technology using special gossip, it’s one of the most promising platforms?
- Of course, walking and constantly holding a smatophone in raised hands isn't very comfortable, but I don't think: the most powerful option would be a "transportable" form of real estate expansion, when it would be possible to swirl at any time from the glasses to a smartphone, a projection screen, and further round.
Glasses - it is a very personal device, this is their strength (primary things you only see), and at the same the same time weakness - augmented reality on the basis of spectacles does not allow you to work on virtual objects at the same time with other people.
Recognize for a moment that manipulating virtual holographic objects in the air is available not only to Tony Stark in "Iron Man", but also an ordinary mortal.
There's one problem that critics often point out: there's no touch response!
Hands don't feel anything!
What answers are your group preparing for this challenge?
- In my lecturations, I often say that augmented reality - this is the seventh consecutive breakthrough in the interaction between humans and the machine.
Replenishing tactile sensations could easily become an eighth breakthrough.
In the meantime, it is used one of the interesting tricks - the use of the other hand as a certain kind of background for imaging.
In an unparalleled way, it records compression!
But there are also technologies that are really aimed at adding palpability to these "images in the air" - for example, the interference of several directional ultrasonic rays at the defined point where the finger is located evokes a palpable feeling, but it is still very weak - as if you were blowing on your tummy fingers.
There is also a wrist bracelet, which affects the nerve endings in the fingers - also a promissible direction.
Have you tried to deceive your brain?
So that he thinks he feels what he seems to feel when he sees something?
- That's a good idea, we haven't tried it yet.
Here there is another challenge that will be fought for for a long time - how to get a person who is physically in a very confiscated space to believe that he walks through an open, practically infinite space - we are also working on the concept of cross-running tracks (not at all, as in clubs), moving platforms and also huge balls.
If misleading of the brain becomes a goal, then work is still for many years to come.
That's what's working on virtual reality so appealing to researchers - a lot of things are in the utter origins.
