Families in space
The country is in jeopardy.
A global warming or colliding with a killer asteroid.
Caravans of cosmic arrondissua with Earthlings on board embark on a journey, looking for a surrogate planet.
In order to conserve humanity, the guiding question arises: how to keep their genus under weightlessness or on a given substitute planet?
I don't think the choice is very great.
In fact, there are only two planets that can be adopted, if only hypothetically.
“It’s Venus and Mars,” says Vladimir Surdin, a high-string scientist at the State Astronomical Institute of Astronomical institute.
However, while there are better conditions for life on Mars, temperatures reach 500 degrees Celsius on the surface of Venus.
It is only possible to live there at a great height or in the orbit of Venus... in the universe.
To address the issues of reproducation in the universe, scientists with flora have begun.
Half a century ago experiments were carried out on the plants.
The four-generation peas cultivated in orbit are no different from their earthly relatives.
After that it was rendered insects into orbit - a very small Drosophila fruit flies.
A quail equine was sent into space in 1979 in order to verify how an embryo develops in a state of weightlessness.
At the ascension we get an absolutely normal dick.
But then the problems begin.
"It arises a problem with the fact that this bird has to find a support, it has to stand on its own feet and start to move," says Vladimir Sychchev, head of the Institute of Biomedical Problems (IBMP).
If they don't find support, the birds are messingly falling and doing somersaults.
After 10 o'b, there was a complete disappearance of instincts in the newly hatched birds.
The birds didn't respond to light or sound.
And the problem was that within four days they just perished.
"Twice we've hatched birds, but then we turned it down because it's just impossible to work with them there," comments Vladimir Sychrev.
The last "miniarch" with the animals flew into orbit 16 years ago.
In the spring of 2013, the experiments will continue.
In the biosatellite "Bion", however, there will only be beings of one gender.
There has been an experiment with rats who were already pregnant in space.
Basically, they didn't find anything supernatural.
"It wasn't just for biosatellites, but again - it is a unique experiment and such experiments need to be carried out," says Vladimir Sychev.
After landing on the ground, cosmic rats gave birth to offspring.
However, it has not so far been approaching the problem of procreation directly in the universe.
It's a difficult task.
At the loss of the usual environment, animals simply can not realize their sexual instinct.
Man, in contrast to animals, in principle he can.
Homo sapiens dominates abstract thinking and is able to create an adequate emotional background.
For Ethical reasons, similar experiments are not carried out.
But women have been flowing into space for 50 years.
There was the greatest danger to the Těreškov.
The pristine thing that humanity has is a female body.
Our Tchaika flew away and no one on Earth could say whether it would last a flight into space.
Whether he can give birth to a child after such a flight.
"No one has ever responded to this question yet," says veteran Vachtang Vačnadze.
In June 1964, more than a year after flying into space, Valentina Tereshkova gave birth to the first wife-cousan co-couster Valentina Tereshkova.
Cosmonaut Andrijan Nikolayev was also the father of the child.
In 1988 he gave birth to the son of a second female cosmonaut Svetlana Savická, who visited the orbit twice and who also worked in an open universe.
However, the risk remains.
We only have a few cosmonauts who, after many years, have been born and healthy offspring.
"Moreover, it can be dangerous even in orbital flights," says cosmonaut-pilot, The Hero of the Soviet Union and Russia's Heroine Valerii Poljakov.
Nevertheless, humanity must inevitably look for new ways of biotechnology, radiological protection and the formation of artificial gravity.
Hydro-laboratory CPK is a mandatory stage of flight preparation.
Here, with zero-attraction conditions, cosmonauts are acquiring the habits needed to work in an open universe.
Water micites a state of weightlessness.
Although for adults water is even a comfortable, but in a way a strange environment, for young children it is a natural element.
Little ichtiander seems to confirm life on the earth comes from the ocean.
There is a connection between the fact that the baby is on an average of 9 months in my mother's amniotic fluid and that it is then easier for them to accude to the water.
In principle, it is logical, because from the moment of birth, from the moment of the first redemption, only 2 weeks have elapsed.
"It's very little for a child to forget something," says baby bathing instructor Marina Aksenova.
In short - if the state of weightlessness for the newborn baby is a more natural state, then for women there is a more naturally inevitable gravity, an attractive force.
In a state of weightlessness occurs very quickly to withrhalation of the abdominal musculature, the pelvic muscles of the pelvis, the body's ability to expel the fetus decreases.
This can be left aside if stimulators of muscle activity in childbirth work.
After all, childbirth can take place in a special chamber, in which the mother's organism expel the fetus.
"And what's next?" Valerij Poljakov asks complex questions.
On the other hand, the baby also needs artificial gravity.
As long as the gravity doesn’t affect the body, it can’t properly form a skeleton or a system of muscles.
It is not possible to dress a newborn in an old-fashioned radii as an adult in a special suit with a burden in which he could train.
It will simply be deprived of what he unconditionally needs for his development.
"If we go with you into an experiment that will admit the birth of a child in a foreign environment, we will bring a disabled person on Earth who will be completely unadapted to the local environment," argues Igor Pestov, the head of the Bio-ethics Commission for IBMP bioethics.
So the birth of children in the universe today is just a theoretical question.
But one day there will come a time when it will become a reality, and when the Earthwarves in their congregations embark on a distant planet, which will become the home of their descendants born in the universe.
Head of the SAO: Scratch system control has failed through the fault of the politicians
In connection with public contracts, the Czech Republic has good inspectors and high-quality legislative, but it is lagging in its application.
In an interview for Aktuálně.cz, Miloslav Kala, vice-president of the Supreme Audit Office, says this.
"The law will never be perfect, but its application has to be correct - that's what in my opinion is lacking in my opinion," Kala said.
Similar conclusions indicate a joint inspection of Czech and German controllers.
As an example of improper practice he cites Petr Nečas's approach to the so-called "švarc system".
Recently, the Prime Minister stated that the ODS will not harass entrepreneurs with its controls - is it therefore prohibited or allowed?
"After all, the law must be set up somehow, and if it disables something, even the prime minister can not prevent the activities of the office, which has the obligation to go to control and enforce duties," Kala said.
Speaking at the meeting of the Audit Committee in the Chamber of Deputies, you talked about a joint project between the Czech and the German side, within which legislation on public procurement in both countries was compared.
What specifically was it about?
It's a Parallel check, which we started about two years ago.
In simple terms, European legislature regulates how to deal with public deliveries, followed by individual national legislation and then practice itself.
We put it all together, and even if the control is not yet finished, we have shown very interesting differences - in general, the legislation in our country is perhaps "tighter and more perfect", but the practice itself is better in some parameters in Germany.
Therefore, it confirms that it is not enough to constantly create more and more precise rules, but it is necessary to deal with the application of the law itself.
Why can this project help you, what is going on for it for you?
Precisely such joint control could contribute in order to stop efforts to refine the laws, reduce and improve the limits when it does not have such a positive impact.
Efficiencies means gaining an appropriate thing for a reasonable (which does not always mean the lowest) price in order to exclude enrichment and possibly criminal conduct.
But by lowering the order limit, we don't necessarily have to achieve that.
Perhaps the system will override with so much "paper", that those who want to look for holes in the system will draw from it much more easily than if the limit remained higher.
You are talking about domestic problems transgressing legislation related to public procurement into practice.
How does the control system in Germany work?
Is there an office similar to our SAO, or is it set up differently?
Regarding the authorities, just as the SAO works there Bundesrechnungshof, which is organized similarly to us, it also has a collegium, it is only formed in a slightly different way, but in principle the two authorities are advising similarly.
Also, the powers are to a certain extent similar, but by the fact that Germany has a federal arrangement, so it has these auditorial courts even at the level of individual Länder - in which the system differs a little from ours.
BRH can only control federal money, represented by state funds.
Public funds, which are managed by regions and municipalities in our country, are inspected from the level of Länder's accounting courts.
As far as the legislation is concerned, it is easier than ours?
In general, I wouldn't like to compare it without specific data, but in some respects Germany is considered a role model, but it certainly can not be said that it is better in everything.
Is it also because they have better controllers?
Definitely don’t, but again I say, I don’t like comparisons.
But it has to be said that even the countries that we consider a role model are faced with a number of problems.
Otherwise it wouldn’t be beneficial for them to cooperate with our office, would it?
Respond to domestic legislation, what did the amendment to the Public Proceedings Act mean to your office, are their impacts already manifested?
Due to the fact that the period of validity of this amendment is relatively short, it is still not reflected in our control actions.
Because we do ex-post controls, we need to reckon with a few delays.
So far we are only observing it in the process of preparing future inspections - we have started our new tool "hazing of risks of non-efficiency", through which we drove almost 14 thousand public contracts, and we have analyzed them - these changes will of course be manifested there, because the limits will change, the conditions of some types of tender will be adjusted, and so on.
Approval of this law, therefore, do you rather consider a benefit or an additional burden on the bureaucracy system?
I don't think this law is definitely a step in the right direction, and I hope it's confirmed.
A problem that can occur here that those rules "stretch" and it won't be possible to follow them.
Already under the functioning of the previous rules, inspected persons are committed by the provider (e.g. in the case of regional operational programmes by the regional council) that any non-compliance with the Public Procurement Act constitutes a breach of budgetary discipline.
Is it worth tightening the law in this way?
I don't think that way.
The system must exclude those who want to attack and abuse it and not punish those who commit formal miscreation that have no impact on decision-making.
Such a system will only put more pressure on bureaucracy.
So how to get out of it?
Let's show where the path doesn't lead.
Recently the Prime Minister Said that the ODS will not annoy entrepreneurs by controlling the so-called "švarc system" - and what does it mean?
Shark system is prohibited or permitted?
After all, the law must be set up somehow, and if it disables something, even the prime minister cannot be prevented in the activities of the office, which is obliged to go to control and enforce duties.
He could say, "We're going to change the law and we're going to allow it", but he can't say we're going to act like nothing.
There are relatively strict rules in the Public Procurement Act, how to be formally carried out - which is correct in order to protect a public tender.
But, on the other hand, it's tracological when a candidate with the best offer is only ruled out for formal deficiencies.
The law will never be superficial, but it has to be the correct application - that's what, in my opinion, is lacking.
The roads are sometimes frosty, but mostly passable
In several places in the Czech Republic the roads are frosted and also snow-covered.
However, roads are usually passable, somewhere with extra caution.
the Karlovy Vary Region
In the Karlovy Vary region this morning the roads were passable, but sometimes frosted and also snowy.
The temperature fell between five to ten degrees below zero, but it is expected to warm slightly during the day.
The snowfall in the region has ceased, and in the lowlands lies only a vague layer.
However, there is about 30 centimeters of snow bloom on the ridges of the Ore Mountains.
In some places there was also lower visibility due to foliage, according to data from the dispatching of regional road workers.
R6 expressways and first-class roads are now passable without restrictions in the region.
Cauzziness, however, is appropriate, for example, on some brispers, where the surface can be frosty and slippery.
All second- and third-class roads, including mountain roads, are also passable.
On them, however, there are still remnants of frozhied and sometimes misguimed snow.
Above all, in higher elevations it is necessary to pay increased attention to driving.
the Pardubice and Hradec Králové Region
On some roads in Eastern Bohemia there is a risk of frizz, in higher elevations and in the mountains there may be a layer of snow on the roads, informs the Directorate of Roads and Motorhomes on its website.
Road workers warn drivers against frost, it can be formed especially in the higher elevations of the Pardubice Region.
Ice can be around Lanškroun, Ústí nad Orlicí, Polička, Svitavy or Vysoké Mýto, especially on second- and third-class roads.
The I/43 and I/34 roads near Svitavy are chemically treated.
Snow is mainly on the roads in the Giant Mountains and the Orlické Mountains.
There is a hidden snow layer in the highest positions on the roads in Rychnov nad Kněžnou and Trutnov districts.
In Eastern Bohemia today it is most often clear to semi-clear without precipitation.
Temperatures usually range between minus three and plus one degrees Celsius, a slight breeze is blowing.
The Pilsen Region
Roads in the Pilsen region are passable this morning, but in places with increased caution and the ride needs to be adapted to the weather.
This is frosty in the morning, the temperatures varies between three to nine degrees below frost.
After previous snowfall and subsequent cooling, some communications can be icy.
In other places, drivers also have to reckon with the fog, but visibility will gradually improve.
This is according to information from the dispatch of regional road workers.
The D5 highway is passable almost without restrictions, but increased caution is recommended by communication administrators between 80 and 131 kilometers.
Most first-class roads have a dry and frost-free surface.
However, there may be frozen sections in the southern Pilsen and Tachov regions.
Second- and third-class roads are moist, and also in this case there is a warning on icy sections.
Drivers should be especially careful on less busy roads in the Šumava region.
the Olomouc Region
Snow on the road must now be reckoned by drivers pointing to the highest elevations of the Olomouc Region.
After chemical sprinkles, she remained on Červenohorské saddle and on the way in the direction of the Videlský Cross.
Road workers were expelled into the terrain at night by the snowsmi-determined, according to road administrators, about three centimeters were snowed in Šumperk.
In other parts of the county, roads are usually passable unrestricted.
"Sumperks lies in the highest elevations re remnants of snow.
Drivers have to reckon with snow porridge on Červenohorské saddle in the direction of Jeseník," a dispatcher of the Šumperk Road Administration told the Czech News Agency today.
Their colleagues in the Jeseník region also went into the terrain at night, but the highest positions are now bare and wet after the chemical treatment.
In the Olomouc region the roads are passable unrestricted, but in Šternberk, drivers should be wary in forest sections, where the roads remained wet.
Uzbekiness Region, Liberec Region
Several difficult-to-pass places are reported by road workers in the north of Bohemia this morning.
In Addition to several places with incomposed snow or glacier, a mountain road from Telnice to Knínice in the Ústek region is also confined, according to a police database.
Temperatures are held even in lower positions below zero, so the ice and snow probably will last on the roads. In the lowlands, especially southeast of the Central Bohemian Uplands, on the contrary, there are no problems and the roads are usually drenched.
Operational impeccation is not yet reported.
The occurrence of the glacier is reported by road workers, especially from around Štětí.
According to meteorologists, conditions were ideal for her at night - during the day the rain and melting snow, at night bright and frenzy.
Unfavourable conditions are also reported on the I/13 road on the border of the Ústecký and Liberec regions.
Closure from Telnice to Knínice necessosoes were nebulized by bleaching branches of trees that beveled to the pavement under snow.
Šimon Ornest: We want to gain positive energy at concerts
What do you think is the end of the world in less than a month?
It's just one of the other alarm messages that we humans like to catch on.
Together with the band The Tap Tap, we make fun of it and we say that we are the only band in the world who can gather enough positive energy to delay or prevent the end of the world.
In addition, in December you will organize an absolutely unique project three concerts against the end of the world.
Can you introduce it closer to your readers?
It's a nationwide benefit event that we've been practicing for two years.
We have decided to take advantage of the marketing potential of the end of the Mayan calendar announced on December 21 at 11:10 a.m.
The day before, on December 20 at 21 o'CL o'bilic time, 3 concerts will take place concomitantly in Prague, Brno and Ostrava.
They will end roughly at a time when on the island of Kiribati in Quimoria, which is 12 hours ahead of the time zone, the Mayan calendar will end.
Who came up with this idea?
Originally it was probably my idea, then we invented everything together with our artist Honza Augusta.
Apart from the fact that we want to accumulate enough positive energy and prevent the end of the world, together with the public, we also want to reflect on the state in which we will one day pass this world on to our children.
On the occasion of the end of the Mayan calendar, we have also created a unique collection of objects of shoes, T-shirts, bags or original sproutings against the end of the world whose purchase at www.e-tap.cz people can also support us.
Together with other performers, the group The Tap Tap also sang the so-called anthem against the end of the world, which is called "The end of the world abolished".
It's already very popular on YouTube, will it also be played at these benefit concerts?
Of course, at the very end, i.e. if we can do everything and the end of the world does not occur...
It will be sung by all performers concomitantly.
Hymns suddenly intertwine in a unique live broadcast on Czech Television.
Words for this song have been written by Tomáš Hanák and the main role of Jesus in the clip is played by Tomáš Hanák.
How did you come to work with them?
We also cooperate with other personalities of the Czech cultural scene, due to the fact that we are organizing a lot of benefficial events and concerts...
We try to intensively involve them in these projects.
It turns out that most of them are interested and entertained by cooperation with us.
What's going to be the proceeds from concerts against the end of the world?
On the equipment of the barrier-free educational center Studeo, which we have been implementing for the sixth year with the civic association Tap in Jedlick's institute.
The teachers regularly come to the students of Jedličková’s intuition and they are engaged in activities that they enjoy and are interested.
Publses themselves don't have the means to go to the lecturers on their own, so we try to enable them in this way.
As part of the completion of Jedlick's institute in the future, a separate building will be created to which we will move with this project.
There will be several musical groups and performers at each concert.
How did you choose them?
We have tried to put together a program that will appeal to all generations, including children.
For example, Chinaski, Support Lesbiens, Illustratosphere with Dan Bárta, The Tap Tap, Marián Bango and Jiří Suchý will perform in Prague.
You can find all the details at www.kpks.cz.
Are you planning any similar "bombastic events" in the future?
For the first time in May we will perform at the Prague Spring for the first time, so we will definitely prepare an interesting program with interesting guests.
In the next year we would also like to play in the Czech National House in New York, and personally, when we are in the U.S., I'd like to connect it with performances in Washington and Chicago.
Your foreign plans aren't minimi-saminy, you've already performed, for example, in Madrid, Brussels, London or Moscow.
However, The Tap Tap is a group consisting of handicapped people.
How do you cope with these paths logistically and organizationally?
It is not as bad as it seems at first glance.
We've got five members in an electric wheelchair, its trolleys have to travel in the luggage compartment, then, of course, we're dragging a large amount of cargo and crates with tools...
However, so far it has always been handled without any problems, CSA and British Airways have been well-prepared for us that I was astonishing many times.
Even in Moscow, from which we recently came back, it was without any problems.
Thanks to trips abroad, you can compare how they differ in barrier-freeness, public access to the handicapped and the like.
What have you encountered so far?
After Madrid, Luxembourg, London and other places where everything works better than in our country, it is in Russia that we have witnessed that in the east everything is still in the very beginning.
Compared to Prague, Moscow is very barrier-free, here it is still unusual for a person in an electric wheelchair to move alone in the city center.
There is no such self-evident there, such as that you prefer wheelchair users in an elevator.
Luckily, civic associations are already being established, trying to draw attention to the problems of people with disabilities.
On the contrary, how do we still have to catch up on more developed countries?
There's a lot of things where we're still behind...
However, it is important to note that the improvement and improvement of the situation is always dependent on the efforts of the people involved.
It is quite common in London and Madrid that even people with severe disabilities move independently in the public, they can go to the toilet, to a museum, anywhere...
However, it is not very customary that a large number of people with disabilities are actively participating in social life there, on the contrary, we are a little ahead with The Tap Tap!
Responsibility of the public or barrier-freeness is one thing, but it is only at the moment when important athletes, artists, actors, politicians or lawyers, things really begin to change.
So far, these are isolated cases of people who have a strong will.
The band The Tap Tap is very popular at the moment, but let's go back a few years, what prompted you to set it up in 1998?
As an educator, I joined Jedliček’s institute, where I had a lot of young people around me who were interested in paying attention to something.
And since I'm a musician myself, among other things, I'm playing a saxophone, together with a colleague, I founded an interest music club.
Over time, as our moderator Láďa Angelovič says, got a little out of hand (laughs).
But the popularity came only in the last few years, or am I wrong?
It is true that it helped us when we began to connect with well-known singers and also actively work to promote the band.
We've realised a job that can't be seen, it's basically as if it didn't exist.
Thanks to contributions from the European Union we can also afford high-quality lecturers, equipment and so on.
Was it your goal to do it with The Tap Tap like this high?
From the beginning I felt the potential to do something differently.
Showbusiness is cluttered with things where one mimiles the other.
In a way, it is logical, everything new is received cautiously and it lasts a long time.
There are very few things that are unique, but I dare to argue that Tap Tap is one of them.
The first impression in a person who sees us for the first time is, of course, remorse, that's natural...
But the repercussion is utterly superfluous, because people with a handicap are not suffering from all-ai-eaten beings we have to repay.
These are people who can live a full-fledged life and develop, of course, assuming they have the necessary means to do so.
I'm saying, if there's something going to happen to people with disabilities, it's not just a shift just for them, it's for the whole society.
Did it also help this success that you're a sturse boss, as a lot of people have labeled you?
When we want to do high-quality work, we have to be uncompromised in many things and demand a certain discipline.
I think that's perfectly normal.
Some people come to us with a romantic idea and with their heads in the clouds, and when they find that they have to rehearse twice a week, go to the preparation and spend a lot of time on the road for concerts, enthusiasm quickly leaves them.
But that's how it works everywhere, in every group that wants to work and who wants to achieve something.
At the same time, the band The Tap Tap has twenty members.
How many of them were at its beginnings in 1998?
Only one Láďa Angelovich.
We are an open group, people are leaving and coming, that cannot be avoided.
Those who have the willpower and taste always have their doors open
It will take place the day before the expected end of the world, on Thursday 20.12.2012 from 21 o'clock
It will take place in Prague Incheba arena, Brno Fléda and Ostrava Plynojem with the participation of 12 bands and other musicians from the Czech Republic
At the end of the Evening, all three towns will merge with telebridge while chanting the song of the band The Tap Tap "The end of the world canceled".
The aim of the concert is to collect funds for the equipment of the multifunctional barrier-free educational center STUDEO at the Jedlička Institute in Prague in the amount of CZK 25 million
