Poland and Cosmos and Cosmos.
In the previous week, at a meeting of the ministers of the European Space Agency, Poland was accepted as a twentieth member of this European space agency as the second country from the countries of the former Eastern bloc (after the Czech Republic, which became a full-fledged member of ESA on November 12, 2008).
Poland started close cooperation with ESA in 1994 and over the next few years participated in a number of the agency's projects.
The journey of Poland to the cosmos began much earlier.
Polish enthusiasts took pride in cosmos flying before World War II; but they didn't always meet with understanding each other.
I will commemorate, for example, the lecture of A. Šternfeld in the Warsaw Astronomical Observatory, who on December 6, 1933 presented ideas from his pioneering work Entrepreneurship Entrance to the Cosmonautics.
The ideas of a young engineer (born 1905) left listeners cold and after years Šternfeld reminisced that only Dr. Jan Gadomski was interested in his work.
Sternfeld won a Robert Esnault-Peltere and André Louis Hirsch Award for Joining Cosmonautics in France in 1934.
Remitted Dr. Jan Gadomski (1899 - 1966) later became a great promoter of astronomy and cosmonautics.
He has published hundreds of articles in Polish magazines and wrote a number of books devoted to these scientific disciplines.
Gadomski became world-renowned propagator of cosmonautics and his contribution was also appreciated by the fact that one crater on the averted side of the moon was named by his name.
Already in 1925 there was a rail drainage in Poland, which was supposed to be equipped with a rocket engine.
Unfortunately, the author of the proposal is not known and the details of the project are not known.
It is unclear whether the racket should have been used to start the russens or its braking.
Information about this dressage is known only from contemporary printing.
Since 1933, Polish artillery has been dealt with missile missiles.
The research was carried out by Komórka Techniki Uzbrojenia in cooperation with Prof. Mieczyslaw Wolfk and prof. Gustawe Mokrzyckiegi.
It is clear from the survivable materials that the research has reached the stage of practical tests.
However, with the arrival of German troops, the research was interrupted.
In 1937 there was a proposal for a photo-electrical fighter missile, which was designed by engineer Rohoziński and in the following year the work of Rakieta - torpedo powietrzna and rakiotombo losnicza by the author Leliw-Krzywoblock.
Both projects were intended for the military use of missile engines.
Just before the war, the Temporary Scientific Consultative Committee (Tymczasowy Komitet Doradczo-Naukowy), which he coordinated all the work, was devoted to all projects of military use of missile technology.
The committee was founded in 1937, but after a two-year activity its activity ceased the start of the war.
Further works dedicated to the cosmonautics appear after the war in the Polish press thanks to the work of the Polish Astronautic Society (Polskie Towarzystwo Astronautyczne).
For the first time it is a mention of the company in the November issue of the 1954 edition of the 1954 magazine, in which there are four more comprehensive articles devoted to astronautics.
In one of them, from prof. M. Subotowicz is designed to create a company that will deal with cosmonautics.
At that time, projects of artificial satellites were already underway, and it was clear that space research was a prospective field.
From the beginning of 1956 the Polish Astronautical Society (PTA) has been trying to enter the International Astronautical Federation (founded in 1951) and already in the autumn PTA became a proper member.
And in the following this year, the first PTA chairman Kazimierz Zarankiewicz (1902 - 1959) became vice-president of the International Astronautical Federation.
He held this position until his death in 1959.
Since 1956, PTA has significantly participated in the development of a very successful meteorological rocket RM (Rakieta Meteorologicna), which became the first Polish rocket to carry out scientific research.
The first RM-1 was completed in 1957 and the first launch took place on October 10, 1958.
The skyrocket with an interval of 1800 meters was about 80 cm long and its weight was less than 5 kg.
Later, an improved RM-1A was erected, and in the summer of 1959 flight tests of the two-degree RM-2 rocket began in the Bledov desert.
The racket was 1.4 meters long and its weight was about 11.5 kg.
Another developmental type should already be capable of real scientific work - the RM-34 rocket was supposed to have an output of up to 14.5 km and its task was to monitor the high-altitude wind.
However, further developments were suspended in 1962.
The Meteor-1 rocket developed from 1962 to 1965.
The racket was conceived as a two-degree, its total length was 510 cm and the starting weight was 32.5 kg.
Threathed Meteor-1A, -1B and -1C have been developed), which differed in the size of space for scientific instruments.
In the Meteor-1A rocket there was an area of 0.4 litre, Meteor-1B with 0.34 litre and Meteor-1C with a volume of 0.62 liters.
The maximum attendance for all three types was 37 km.
Between 1965 and 1968 there was the development of the Meteor-2 rocket at the Aviation Indigestion, whose first flight tests began in October 1970.
The Meteor-2 rocket had a starting weight of 380 kg and it was capable of executing a payload weighing 10 kg to a height of around 60 km.
Subsequently, Meteor-2H and Meteor-3 versions were built.
It is also necessary to mention Poland's accession to COSPAR (Committee for Space Research) in 1960 and the creation of COSPAR national committee two years later.
As part of the Intercosmos program, Poland also participated in space exploration on Soviet artificial satellites, and in 1978 Polish pilot Miroslaw Hermaszewski became the second inter-cosmonator after Vladimir Remko.
Repeal of the law on public works is not a solution.
In the last week, the Constitutional Court annoyed the Public Works Act.
The resolution provoked a vivid debate in the public.
It will certainly be interesting to look at the issue from a broader point of view.
Liberal-centric economic systems in the EU, as in a globalized world, are based on the principle of multi-regited, unregulated competition.
In its effect, this means that individual economic departments, national economic systems, stand in a perpetually conflict with each other.
The cause is the principle of free commerce and the unresponsive movement of private capital along with unbridling financial speculation.
Due to the considerable differences in the price of labor, (chaom) there is a price tension.
It is necessary to understand the situation where the manufacturer strives by importing cheap goods into competition by "stacking" the price of a competitor in order in order to bring a greater market share to each other, thereby increasing its own gains.
On a massive scale, this means for the majority of entrepreneurs to either rescheduled production abroad, or to buy there cheaply or to perish. The result is high unemployment in countries where labor prices are high compared to other economies.
Since private capital does not have any social responsibility and thus the responsibility for its produced unemployment, the social expenditure of the state necessarily grows.
The whole situation is amplified by the complete reluctance of the business owners to pay taxes and thus to replace the economic and social society-wide damage caused in the pursuit of profit.
Situation is so well-argued that there is no need to provide specific statistical data.
Unmistakable private-primary practices in individual economies create a situation where national governments are compelled to enter into mutual competition in order to artificially reduce the social level of their own population in order to attract foreign capital into the country.
Inherently, governments give private-primary capital their own population, regardless of the social slumb.
This is mainly reflected in changes to applicable laws.
The aim is to economically foreclosure private-prime a price dictate, especially in the area of wages.
This takes place, on the one hand, by a system of economic computation in the event of long-term unemployment and by limiting the rights of workers in the field of labour law.
As a result, rising poverty, along with an increase in disparities between the poor and the rich.
There have been public dispensaries of food for the poor in Germany for many years who cannot make a living by their own work.
The number of such people is already going into the millions.
As an increase in the competitiveness of the German economy, there are quite commonly instances that duly-employed people receive such a salary that the state must pay them to an existential minimum.
Such a scandal was also published in the case of auxiliary employees of the federal assembly.
Undoubtedly, the austerity measures of virtually all southern EU states lead to the same situation when people, under the pretreaching catastrophic slump of the social level, are forced to either emigrate as it did in the 19th century, or to live on the fringe on the margins of society in the hope that private investment capital may one day come to the country.
We have to ask where it will come from?
If it is from other EU countries, then poverty will move from one country to another, or it does not come at all, because the Chinese, Ind, the Brazilian, or the Turk, the Moroccan, or the Egyptian, or an African are still working for a fraction of European salaries.
This applies to all of Latin America.
Poliary theories and the media misbehavior alleges that the state cannot capitalize on its own economy, that a managed economy leads to an economic catastrophe.
Equiroidal capital insists on the opinion that the state must not interfere in the economy by directing interventions.
Then it is necessary to ask whether private capital does not influevolving, now it actually governs politics and thus the whole state in its own egoistic sense.
Here's an unequivocal answer that it does.
Evidence is the existence of virtually omnipotent and ubiquitous lobbies in all states.
