MVRDV architects show that real adventures don’t just take place in the head—such as Spijkenisse and the recently-established Mountain of Books
"The building seems hilarious to me, it looks futuristic. At last you can see something interesting again," says Listette Verhaig, who walks around on the side of the road.
Stefan Spermon, an IT technician in a nearby big business, says: "Of course, the house is nice."
However, I wonder why we still need the library for today.
They all have internet, iPads and e-books.
No one voluntarily goes to any of these old-currencil libraries today, do you?
Spijkenisses, a housing estate in close proximity to Rotterdam without its own job opportunities, which is not even worth a visit, holds a special record.
This municipality with 80 000 inhabitants exhibits the lowest rate of education in the whole of the Netherlands.
In order for this stupid situation to reconsider a few years ago the decision to contribute to universal education and to build seven nicely painted reinforced concrete miniatures of fictitious bridges, which are depicted on euro banknotes.
However, the success of an educational offensive was not dizzying.
And so the city councillors recognized that there is only one way to become masters of statistics: we have to have a library!
Winy Maas from the MVRDV architectural studio in Rottredam, a master of insanely daring column graphs and a contractor of witty, often even cynical buildings, accepted the thing with the usual calmness, and at a competition meeting in 2003 he appeared with five books under his arm and a smirk on his face.
And while the commission was still stunned and shruggling his shoulders slapped, the cheeky Maas piled his presents on himself according to his size and ended his activist-reasoned speech with the words "Dear City Community!"
So this is my suggestion for the Mountain of Books in Spijkenisse, the so-called Boekenberg!
Nine years later, for 30 million euros the mountain was piled up.
It is a part of the revitalizing project, which also includes an underground parking lot, a supermarket, a post office and several adjacent residential and terraced houses with a total of 50 apartments.
At the beginning of November, the Mountain of the Books was awarded the second place "Best Library of NL 2012".
In addition, the project is nominated for the Dutch National Wood Award 2012.
Small-urban gray, which has not yet been able to offer more than a post-modern pedestrian zone and an extraordinarily nasty town hall, behind whose white façade you would rather expect a dairies, is richer by a little contemporary architecture.
Above all, Spijkenisse now has the first public cultural structure in the history of its existence.
The Long Path to the Book
First Impression: reading eldorado under the hatch for cheese.
An example of a massive mountain is driven by an elevator, but you will not discover the real spatial and reading passions only during a pedestrian overcoming of topography.
From all sides the glazied interior is bright and well-arranged, the floor made of burnt cabbage and an elegant street lantern clearly express the atmosphere of a public square.
The city's environment is perfect.
You can't see park benches, dogs and boys and girls who play football.
Everywhere books, books, books.
"Normally there are shelves with books along the façade and in the middle there is a large dark space, which is mostly uncoyant and impersonal," Winy Maas says.
We built a classic spatial configuration upside down, and the space for the reader was ejected from the inside.
The interior of the Mountains of books is cleverly utilized: in the center there are offices, an internet library, a chess club, an ecological center and a control room of household equipment.
Strangely pleas are black shelves for books, which are once the lining of the walls, one sill and at other times the handrails of the stairs.
Appearance, structure and odor have a risk.
Even sworn architects and structural engineers tumbling their heads over an unfamiliar building material.
"We wanted to work here with recycled materials," says Joop Trouborst, the project manager at Spijkenisse's municipality.
And so, in a fries farm, we suddenly came across a suitable waste product from agriculture.
For many years it has been used as a base under the roots of a millimetre fleece made of synthetic matter in greenhouses and in the fields in the Netherlands.
It is inexpensive and time efficient.
Threy textiles last one, two seasons and is ripe for dissupositive waste.
For the library, the fleece - for the first time in such a quantity - was pressed on plates with a thickness of four centimeters.
Through the action of heat and pressure, the so-called Landbouwplastic (KLP) turns into a dark, homogeneous and load-begby material that stinves a bit like a new car and a bit like sports shoes.
After the 105 steps you can get to the top.
At the end of an almost 500-metre-long pilgrimage, you will be rewarded not only with a fantastic view of the city, as well as Dutch croquettes and fiskes in flower pots.
They take care of the atmosphere, but mainly about the correct humidity of the air in the mountains of literature.
Gifts for a new soul
"You probably wouldn't believe it, but in spite of many glass surfaces, this house is an exemplary project in terms of ecology," Trouborst says.
