Szabo: "The Germans must play a bigger role"
Germany refrained from a vote in arranging Palestine.
According to U.S.-Peter affairs expert Stephen Szabo, Berlin is teeming on the narrow edge of diplomacy.
At the beginning of the week, Germany first signaled that it would vote against the proposal for Palestinian observer status at the United Nations.
Berlin abstained from the poll.
- Why ?
Stephen Szabo: Germany doesn't support what the Israelis did in Gaza.
Now, however, Germany must be prudish because of its particular relationship with Israel.
But at the same time, I don't even think they're going to support the U.S. position.
Germany wanted to demonstrate its independence - without being, of course, too critical of Israel.
During the uprising in Libya in March 2011, Germany also abstained when it came to establishing a no-fly zone.
In the end, it was enforced by NATO.
Is it difficult for Germany to take a clear stance on serious international issues?
Yes, that's the case.
The reason is that it is precisely changing its foreign policy, towards German foreign policy.
It is all the more difficult because Europeans do not have a coherent and unified policy.
Therefore, the Germans are between two queues.
They are expected to play a more independent role, but they aren't accustomed.
I don’t think they’re just settling in their role, but they’re on their way to “more normal” foreign policy.
To the foreign policy they have in France or the United Kingdom.
Specifically, what does "Normal" foreign policy mean from a German point of view?
It demonstrates the readiness in the event of national interests and attitudes that are independent of the positions of the United States or European partners.
I think that German foreign policy is motivated by an economic policy, that is, exports and its relations with certain areas, such as Russia, China or the Middle East.
Germany's economic interests are to a certain extent different from the economic interests of other superpowers, and that is why Germany must protect its interests.
Did economic interests have an influence on the Middle Eastern conflict and the vote at the United Nations?
First of all, Germany has large outlets in the Middle East, especially in the countries around the Persian Gulf.
Therefore, it must be careful not to deteriorate the public, but also elites in the Arab lands.
In any case, it plays a role.
But I wouldn’t even want to attach too much importance to it, that’s not exactly a one-sided relationship.
In any case, it plays an important role in German deliberation.
Did Germany's relations with the U.S. be injure when Germany abstained from a vote in the case of both important decision-making, for example when voting on Palestine?
I think there is a great sympathy in Europe and even in the U.S. for a German stance.
That's why I don't think it's been such a dramatic turning point as on Libya.
Perhaps Germany will even benefit some respect from it.
This indicates that it has to be regarded as an international player and their interests must be taken into account.
There are differing views on the Palestinian initiative in Europe.
On the other hand, the U.S. has made a clear vote for the veto.
Are there disparities between the U.S. and many European nations?
These divergences have always existed as a result of U.S. internal politics.
I don't think that the Obama administration has a secretly understanding of the European stance.
But as a result of the political situation there, of course, it can't defend this stance publicly.
I don’t really think the differences in opinions really aren’t as big as they always seem.
If you look at relations between Obama and Prime Minister Netanyahu, Obama isn't very imputeed by Netanyahu's policies at all.
Isn't it difficult for Germany to reconcile its close ties to Israel and the U.S , on the one hand, and the position of the most important partners in the EU on the other?
I think that's exactly what makes it more difficult for the Germans.
Of course, it would be a little easier for the Germans if there was a coherent and unequaintive European policy, which is not happening at the moment.
Therefore, they can not be a part of a wider line and instead have to drive things forward from their own position.
That's what they do with the euro.
I believe that in the future Germany will assume a leading role and push Europe towards a European stance.
Of course, it is not easy for Germany because of the relationship with Israel.
It has always been a susceptible topic.
However, I think it is clear to the Germans that they have to play a more independent role.
Doesn't Germany see itself as an important international player? Does it even want to take a major role?
Or is the leading role for Germany still difficult?
Germany is still not accustomed to it, it is still annoying, and for obvious reasons it is still difficult to play a bigger role.
When, for example, we look at the European crisis, every time Germany takes a bigger role, anti-German sentiment arises.
It doesn’t impurprise the Germans.
It's actually an old problem: we don't want to be surrounded by hostile countries.
From this point of view, Germany is in a much more difficult stance than the U.S.
It has to be acceptable for all sorts of neighbors and opinions, and that's not easy.
U.S. influence in European politics is steadily declining, but the EU does not currently fill this vacuum.
The Germans will just have to play a bigger role.
Even if they don't like it, they're annoying, and they're going to make them even more unfamiliar - c'est la vie!
Stephen Szabo is Deputy Director of the Transatlantic Academy in Washington, an institute where academies and policy experts from Europe and North America jointly study the challenges of the Transatlantic Commonwealth.
Szabo is also a member of the German Marshall Fund, specializing in German politics, U.S. foreign policy and transatlantic relations.
