It's not so bad to be bored in a world of constant connectivity
I unexpectedly spent five hours at the airport when our plane had mechanical problems and we had to await the arrival of the next plane.
So I've had a lot of time to think about boredom.
I won't lie to you.
Expecting half a day at the airport for the next flight is pretty an annoying, despite dispersed with books, magazines and an iPhone (not to mention duty-free stores).
But some scientists and experts on children's development are increasingly coming with the praise of boredom.
They say it's good to get bored for us (and our children).
It forces the brain to go into interesting directions, which can promote creativity.
And since most of us are constantly connected to a screen nowadays, we don’t have a chance to enjoy boredom.
So should we promote boredom?
- Yes.
And no.
But I'll come back to that.
First of all, like many people, I assumed that boredom is a relatively new phenomenon that occurred with the arrival of more free time.
But as Peter Toohey, professor of Greek and Roman history at the University of Calgary of Canad, who is the author of Boredom: A Lively History
"Finally, boredom has a long history," he said.
On the walls in Pompeii was found a Latin inscription related to boredom, dating from the first century AD.
And then there is also the question of how we define boredom.
The problem is that it has been defined and debated in many different ways, said John D. Eastwood, a psychology professor of psychology at Ontario, ontario.
After viewing the research literature and demonstrating an idea to the target group of about 100 people, Professor Eastwood and his colleagues defined boredom as an "I wanted to do something that would satisfy me, but I wasn't capable of it".
Boredom differs from apathy in the fact that a person is not involved in anything, but she would like to be.
She says there's no need for apathy to do anything.
He says at the heart of boredom is "disturbance of the process of attility, associated with a bad mood and a sense of slow-running time."
Boredom can be reminiscent of depression.
But Professor Eastwood has said that while they can have a mutual relationship, bored people tend to see the problem as an environment or the world, while people who are depressed see the problem in themselves.
Sometimes we think we’re bored when we’re just having trouble concentrating.
"The Unengaged Mind: Defining Boredom in Terms of Attention", which was published in the journal Perspectives on Psychological Science, Professor Eastwood and his colleagues pointed to an earlier experiment in which attendees obscured the tape on which a person read an article from the journal.
Some groups have heard a loud and unresponsive television program in the next room, others heard it so quietly that they had barely spotted it, while the third group didn't hear it at all.
Those who listened to the televised program quietly reported more boredom than the other two groups - they had difficulty concentrating, but they weren't sure why and they attributed these difficulties to boredom.
Trying to focus on a difficult or engaging culpable culpability can miscarriage lead to boredom, Mark J. Fenske, neuroscient at the University of Guelph in Ontario, and one of the study writers.
On the other hand, when you're doing something dull, "such as finding the wrong parts on a factory line, distracting music can help you don't get boried."
In fact, he said, we now know that wriving and scribbling, which is often viewed as a sign of boredom, can actually help to fight boredom and keep people more physically attentable.
"Expellent shows that children who are allowed to wiggle will learn more and retain more information than those who are being forced to sit still," Professor Fenske said.
We've all experienced boredom sometimes - my flight delay, a rooting loudspocking loudspeaker, a particularly tedious movie.
But some individuals have a better chance of getting bored than others.
To help measure boredom in the 80's of the last century, scientists have evolved a "Shapic for Susceptibility to Boredness" (Boredom Proneness Scale).
Scale includes questions such as: "Many things I have to be done are repeated and monotonous," and "I've got so many interests that I don't have the time to do them all."
Using these scales, the researchers found that boys tend to be bored more often than girls, said Stephen Vodanovich, a psychology professor at the University of West Florida, in particular when it comes to the need to have more and different external stimulations.
Teenagers are generally indifferent.
Reed Larson, a professor for human and societal development at the University of Illinois, conducted an experiment seven to eight times a day contacting nearly 400 tempt and their parents.
He found that 32 per cent of teens said they were getting bored at the school and doing their homework, while 23 percent said they were getting bored when they weren't at school.
On the other hand, 3 per cent of parents said they were getting bored.
Professor Larson said he didn't know whether a percentage statement of boredom would now be, 21 years later, higher or lower.
However, he said that he knew that "growding is a peak time for boredom", which is largely due to the fact that children and the youth do not have much control over what they want to do.
So back to my original question: "Is booness good for you?".
As research shows, sometimes it doesn’t, because, in an extreme form, it can lead people to take absurd physical risks, play gambling or abuse addictive substances as a way to alleviate it.
On the other hand, many philosophies and writers discussed the connection between boredom and creativity, said Professor Vodanovich, who studied the problem for more than two decades.
"Break is a way of brain telling you that you should do something else," said Gary Marcus, a psychology psychology professor at NYU.
But the brain doesn’t always know the best thing you should do.
If you are bored and use this energy to play guitar and cooking, it will make you happy.
However, if you watch the TV, it will only make you happy for a while, not for the long term.
So if your child is bored and you give him an iPad, he won't be bored anymore, but he won't learn how to confiscate himself or drive independently, Professor Fenske said.
And "this self-government is transmitted from one situation to another," the professor said.
Your child does not only learn how to entertain himself, but he will gain greater self-control in other areas.
I don’t think we really want to glorify boredom.
We shouldn’t even be too critical of it.
Rather, our goal should rather be to feel comfortable when we are out of the reach of the constant tumbling of activity and technology.
Professor Eastwood agreed.
"We acclause her of the need to bore herself even more, but the boredom is a painful, restless desire to be associated with something meaningful," he said.
What people are actually looking for, he said is a way to relax and enjoy their free time.
"In an environment in which we're constantly over-stimulated," he said, "it's hard to find ways to have fun when the noise shuts down."
