Tashirtisome craft in the world: sulphurous carriers in the area of Kawah Ijen volcano
For four euros gamblers gamble carriers in an Indonesian volcano with their lives and salutes 70 kilo sulphuriage on steep stone paths.
There are people for whom their work is hell, and there are those who literally work in hell.
This is the case of Ant Wijay, one of the 400 miners who make a living by convicting sulphuric sulphuri of Kawah Ijen volcano in the east of the Indonesian island of Java.
Because of this, every day they have to fiddle to the bottom of the crater, escaping sulphurous gases from the interior of the Earth and, when in contact with the air, passes into a solid state.
After slashing large sulfate blocks, weighing up to 70 kilos together, he carries them on the shoulders in two bamboo baskets along steep stone paths.
It's only 250 meters to the top of the volcano, which rises 2 386 meters, but it takes over 40-minute-exhausted carriers to climb the turquoise at a pace, as they maintain a balance and thoroughly decay their footsteps so that they don't smash and crash into the abyss.
They know that any stumping could cost them their lives, as it happened to a French hijacker who crashed Kawah Ijen years ago.
Miners in Kawah Ijen earn five cents for every kilo of sulfur they mine
When they're up there, they're making their way around tourists photographing them as if they're monkeys in a circus, and we're wearily carrying away heavy bins to a vain, which the mining company has placed a little lower at an above-altitude altitude of 1850 meters.
PT Candi Ngrimbi has mined in the volcano since 1960 and has never paid its workers more than 662 Indonesian rupees (five cents) per kilo sulphur.
Then he sells it for 10 000 rupees (83 cents) the petrochemical industry, because this mineral has its place in everyday life and is used for the production of matches, fun pyrotechnics, cosmetics, dynamite and even sugar bleaching.
"Since it's commonly worn $70, we're earning about $46,000 rupees (3.8 euros) for every journey," Anto, who usually completes three journeys a day.
One journey takes him three o'clock and is devastated at the end, but he can earn 138,000 rupees ($11.5) per day.
Even though it seems a negligible amount for such an inevitable endeavor, it's three times what he'd earn in the field.
"Daily wages of miners are very high here. For example, collecting coffee is paid 15 000 rupees (1.2 euros) per day and the salary in a for a fort a for a fortnight is two million rupees (167 euros)," explains the carrier, who previously worked on the tourist island of Bali as a bricklayer.
There was his salute 75 000 rupees (6.2 euros) per day and the work wasn't so hard, but Anto returned with his family to Banyuwangi, a village near a volcano, for a serious reason that's as persuased in Indonesia as sulphagus: "I Married a young girl from Bali who is Hindu, and I've taken her to Java to confite."
Anto has an asthma, it's hard to breathe, doesn't give a damn and his eyes are irritated by toxic gases
At the age of 27, Anto risks his life three times a day in Kawah Ijen, whose sulphuriage has already taken its toll on him, even though his face is protected by a special mask and glasses.
He's got asthma, he's having a hard time breathing, he's always coughing, and his eyes are irritated by toxic gases escaping from a volcano.
That's the price they have to pay to achieve his dream.
“I’m going to work for two more years because I want to open a shop or to study Spanish or French,” he said.
This sympath-of-the-mistaking and intelligent young man chasing fate could be a tourist guide, a waiter or a receptionist in a hotel, and instead he's working as a carrier.
It shares a filthy wooden hut with other carriers and gets up at two o'x O'cate in the morning every day because the sulphuriity doesn't stop pulling out at night, and its characteristic yellow color varies in blue and glows in the dark.
Despite the surrounding darkness, Anto fidgets into the crater and illuminates his path with a small flashlight attached to his helmet and bought it for his own money.
Approximately 400 carriers on the shoulders of a basket with sulphurous sulphuration from the bottom of the crater
Although the company has high incomes, it has not mischanished sulphuric extraction due to cost-saving savings, nor does it not provide carriers working on their own and with such effort, no equipment.
In fact, they don't even receive a share of 30 000 rupees (2.5 euros) out of a surcharge for photography, which, together with a ticket for 15 000 rupees (1.2 euros), are selected by natural park guards from tourists arriving to take a picture of the volcano and its human mules.
"This work is for cattle, and not for humans," argues Madrusin, a sturdy 42-year-old carrier who has been working in Kawah Ijen for three decades, since he left the schools.
He manages up to 110 kilos and assures us that he can withstood "as long as long as it goes" because he needs the money to educate his three sons between the ages of 18 and 10.
I won't retire, I'm going to die here because the volcano was my whole life
Although sulphuriage burns the throat and stubsoths in the eyes when the breeze unexpansed and traps miners in dense fumes coming out of the volcano, the miners are thus arguing that none of them complain that they suffer from serious diseases... but it is evident from their frequent respiratory problems, arthrosis, knee aching pains and wounds on the shoulders caused by the weight of baskets.
Unainik, who balances his basket on his back, can only pay 50 kilos at the age of 53.
Every day he and his colleagues extract 15 tons of sulfur in the volcano, which then take three trucks by a steep route between the thickets to a warehouse in Tamansari, 18 kilometers away.
"I won't re retire, I'm going to die here because the volcano has been my whole life," Unainic declares, opening a broad mouth that lacks several teeth.
Of his five sons, the eldest, a thirty-year-old, also makes money by mining sulfules.
Time goes on, but the sequential misery of one of the heaviest crafts in the world persists from generation to generation - crafts performed by human mules in Kawah Iljen.
“Super-Sugar is looking for Babies to save the economy
The Singaporeans tempt that they don't have children, for careers, stress and livelihood and education costs.
"Singapore population needs to grow."
I'm married, and I'm a landlord, you're my wife and you're a patriot. Let's fulfill our civic culpability and beget a new life!
Unbelievable it may seem that this slogan is a part of an advertisement for mentshol candies, but still - or maybe that's why - the video in Singapore earlier this year has widened en masse on YouTube earlier this year.
These sentences are a part of a rap that uses local allusions such as "we'll put a bun in the oven" as mockery of the birth rate in Singapore.
BBH advertising agency, which created the video, hopes that with this ad they manage to draw attention to the problem in a witty way.
The agency's artistic director Douglas Hamilton says he wanted to take advantage of the influence of music in order for people to fulfill "his civic duty."
"It's exclusively a matter of the Internet, so it must have been funny and funny.
It's the biggest problem in this country.
“We’re the worst thing in the world in reproducing our population, so we’ve had the feeling that it’s a theme we had to touch.
We Knew that the government was attempting for a lot of things to expand perfumes with pheromones or to organize dating parties.
Many of these ideas could have been creative, but not entirely functional.
So we were wondering why not to do the most creative possible thing, like the composition of a rap, to solve this problem.”
1.2 children
Anyone who doesn’t take much of that lightly is a Singaporean government.
They spend $1,300 every year to encourage citizens to have more children.
The government's marriage-familiar/familiar package provides up to $15 000 per child, prolongs maternity leave and allocate financial benefits.
However, all these measures had a small effect.
Singapore is a wealthy city-state with advanced technology in southeastern Asia, also known for the conservatism of its leaders and strict social controls.
The birth rate in Singapore with regard to stratification of the population there are currently 1.2 children per woman.
It last exceeded the birth rate of 2, which is considered a recovery rate, in 1976.
Why don’t Singaporeans have more children?
Tan Wei Ming, the director of the Department of Marriage and the National Population's family, says it's because of "better education" and a "widespread range of career opportunities."
"People thus have a wider range of options when it comes to life goals and priorities, it's not just a wedding and the family," he explains.
These changes in societal standards have contributed to an increase in the number of unmarried people and the postponing of marriage and the procreation of children. The result is a reduction in the birth rate in Singapore.
While within immigration policy there were efforts to drastically increase the influx of immigrants as a measure against a fall in population, it provoked displeasure among local residents.
On some of Singapore's websites, undisguised xenophobic is visible against many new immovable immigrants, especially the Chinese who are criticized for keeping salaries low and making a small effort to integrate.
An increase in Immigration has also been seen as one of the reasons why the ruling Singapore party recorded its worst electoral outcome since the beginning of independence last year.
After the election, there was an effort to solve the problem using quotas and higher fees for foreign workers.
Unexpected Consequences
While a decrease in the birth rate has well-known repercussions on national economic growth, tax revenues, health expenditures and immigration policies, some unexpected consequences have also arisen.
The government is trying not to build so many small houses.
It, for example, began to influence real estate business.
The Office for Urban Development began regulating the number of small apartments known as "shoeboxes", which can be built in certain urban zones.
These apartments have an area of 46 square meters and had a great success in marketability.
Nevertheless, there is a concern that these apartments can support a bachelor lifestyle and discourage developers to build large single-family homes.
But Lim Yew Soon, CEO of EL Developers, argues their "shoeboxes" are sold much faster than larger homes.
"They're more populous, in such a way that they're sold days to weeks faster than houses with a larger area.
That's why they're much better for our cash flow."
Nevertheless, it admits that the new standards set clearer rules to developers who were previously limited when they wanted to offer too many small houses in one project.
Too stressed
Singapore is a city-state.
Although these new rules could lead to an increase in the birth rate of the nation's birth rate, an interview with Singaporeans working in the financial district in the city center suggests that they won't have a big effect.
"Human people are still stressed, houses are expensive, just like an education, that's why a lot of people are postponing it," the young clerk said.
"Others should have children.
But to me, it's important to rely on my own money and time," says another, about twenty-year-old young man.
Both men and women mention their careers, stress and the cost of livelihood and education as the reasons that prevent them from having children.
So, no matter in any way the government tries to force citizens to have their children, it's in the end Singaporeans who have the final say about starting a family.
What's Private Offerquay is Private and Online
The privacy.
According to the diary published by the Royal Spanish Academies, this is something that has a personal nature or an "area of private life, which everyone has the right to protect against any disruption".
What's privacy for a person under the age of 16?
How does he apply this definition in his daily life and on social networks?
Understood the Danger He's being exposed when he publishes matters on the Internet that he certainly wouldn't tell no one during normal communication?
Journalists from elPeriódico interviewed five boys and girls between the ages of 10 and 15, who are frequent Internet users.
In the four cases, they considered the notion of privacy to be "something entirely of their own" on a personal level and a "user's name and a password" when it came to social networks.
“I wouldn’t publish my biggest secret,” Jorge said in an attempt to explain the importance of privacy in places like Facebook, Twitter, Hotmail and Windows Live Messenger, where he’s accounted for two years.
"It's very secret, my mum can know about them, but not the whole world," she says.
"I'm posting nice photos or photos on FB, how we play.
I'm chatting with my audavers, too.
“I wouldn’t share a photo that wouldn’t be mine or a photo to ridicule someone,” she says.
This boy realizes that it is wrong to publish obscene photos, photographs of naked persons or photographs of crimes, or to write humiliating or rude comments.
Jorge says he's familiar with all the 35 friends he's had on FB and his nine supporters on Twitter.
Most of them are relatives.
There's also a mother who has the name and the password of one of the accounts.
"I have set up a Twitter account so I can express my opinions and write interesting posts.
I don’t know if anyone will answer me, I just put them there,” he adds.
"Social networks are fun, I can talk to relatives who are long or quick to communicate with friends," he says.
Without hesitation, he says that he would never accept a request from an undisclosed person.
He wouldn't even be interested if someone recommends someone to an unspoiled.
The fourteen-year-old José's case is vague.
This teen owns accounts on Hotmail, Facebook, My Space and Ask networks, and on the last mentioned network admits that he does not know 20 people from his list of friends.
"I'm not afraid of it because we've got something in common, such as music," he says.
According to this boy, none of them guided him at anything, nor did he want an address or phone number.
"Should they have urged me or demand it from me, I will simply remove them from my account," he admits.
José came forward after he read the recommendation on Twitter.
This boy isn't a stranger's experience with what's now called cyberbullying.
Familiar quinteans were blackmailed on a social network.
Threaten him and demanded money.
He never knew who he was," he confirms.
According to José, the victim did not unregister from the account.
"She's just activating a private regime."
He then explains a series of steps on how to securely set up the account.
Contrary to Jorge, this boy would publish photos of acquaintances in annoying or awkward or awkward situations.
"Of course I'd do it if someone bothered me or I just wanted to.
Even though I'm aware it's cyberbullying," he says.
Crutlique questions
Marielos Porras, an English-graduating teacher and a graduate of Education and Teaching, believes that in order for children and teen in social networks to understand that the main objective of these networks is to inform.
It states that: "The Internet originated as a means to search for information, but when social networks have emerged, the rules of the game have changed."
Marrielos Porras notes that Marc Prensky, a graduate of pedagogy at Yale University and the author of the article Digital Indigenous and Digital Immigration, introduced these concepts to explain the phenomenon.
"Digital Indigenous are children and young people who were born at the age of this technology.
“We’re digital immovable people who have to teach them, and we’re just learning for ourselves,” he said.
He proclaims that it is a complex topic, "because we ask them to recognize what it is appropriate to disseminate, to publish and to say, on the contrary, at an age when they are not yet ripe for it".
“Also, in terms of their surroundings – to be prudent, at a time when what they most crust is to be popular, to have thousands of friends and not to think about the consequences,” he adds.
According to this specialist, it is the most effective way for children and teens to show what privacy it is, asking questions that force them to think.
"Cutting them not to do anything doesn't work," he adds.
Marrielos Porras also enumerates some of the possibilities: Are there things you wouldn't tell a stranger, so why do you do it on the Internet?
“You’d like it if someone posted your picture the way you posted a friend?
Do you know what others are posting about you?
When you refer persons to the photos from the festivities, did you ask these persons for permission?
And one: Do they always need everyone to know what you're doing right now?
The next point is to realize that they have to behave in the same way outside the Internet.
It's all the rules.
"A person outside of the Internet is guided by respect, morality and other principles, and it should be the same on social networks," he said.
Overstell
Estuardo Guardia, university professor, first-degree teacher and educational adviser explains that it is necessary for children's parents to carefully read the rules of social networks.
“Usually insidiousness, they have a good staunsity to talk to their children about the consequences of setting up an account on the Internet.
For example, in terms of the age or what is permissible to share or disclose," he says.
Guardia believes it is crucial to remind children of the traditional "don't talk to strangers."
South America summit closes and Lima statement not disclosed
The Sixth Presidential Campaign of the Union of South American Nations (Unasur) held in Peru ended today, without issuing a Lima Declaration, which was reportedly signed by the seven presidents present.
The Efe news agency has repeatedly attempted to gain access to a document signed at the sixth meeting of the heads of state states and U.s. government, but representatives of the foreign ministry and the presidential bureau, who initially stipulated that they would publish the document after the end of the summit, they said it would later be published on the Peruvian government's website.
Furthermore, they stated that the content was published by Peru's President Ollantou Humala in several short press releases.
Journalists restricted access to information throughout the summit.
During the meeting, a video was transmitted in the press room from the presidential assembly without sound with a "confidential session, the transmission of the sound is prevented".
A small amount of information available to journalists was provided by the press spokespersons of some of the governments, who were present, but not by the Peruvian spokeswomen.
The only document published during the meeting was a list of participating presidents, prompting the displeasure of hundreds of journalists from various national and international media outlets asking for more information.
He later circulated an e-mail to the media with a "final statement" from the summit, but it's just President Humala's remarks, and not an official document that closed the summit.
Last October it hosted Peru's third summit of South American and Arab countries (America del Sur-Países Árabes, ASPA) and even on this occasion the originally promised Lima Declaration was not disclosed, even though it was zealously required by the press.
It can be found on the ASP official website that the document has been published last Tuesday.
At both international sessions, the Peruvian authorities tried to provide broadcasts for all journalists, but to completely prevent the flow of information.
Among other things, the summit ended, among other things, with a joint commitment by Chile and Peru to adopt the judgment of the Hague Court, which resolves a dispute over the border between the two countries.
President of Peru Ollanta Humala and Chile President Sebastián Piñera reunited during a regional meeting, confirming that they would respect the decision of the International Judiciary Court in The Hague, which on Monday will begin by hearing the defense speech of both sides to negotiate a dispute launched by Lima against Santiago.
"We will submit and execute a verdict that will resolve the dispute we are discussing in this international court today," President Humala said along with his Chilean counterpart.
"Chile was and will be a country respectful of international law, peaceful resolution of disputes, treatises and international courts," Piñera added, greeting the President Humala with a hands-to-shape at the flags of the two countries.
Concluding between the two presidents to substantiate the decision of the International Judicial Court has been made cleared after Colombia adopted the Bogot Pact this week, devoting its subjugation to an international court that ruled the maritime border with Nicaragua, and Colombia saw the decision completely flawed.
The summit took place without the participation of presidents of Brazil (Dilma Rouseff), Venezuela (Hugo Chávez), Bolivia (Evo Morales) and Argentine (Cristina Kirchner).
Paraguay was expelled from Unasur in 2011 after then-presiding Fernando Lugo and did not take part.
Host President Ollanta Humala has been commissioned to open morning speeches and conclude a summit in Lima shortly after noon.
The president has read the final document informing that 16 agreements had been taken and 31 projects between South American countries had been planned, with $17 billion investment.
In Addition to adopted agreements, he mentioned that UNASUR member states would take "important steps towards an objective in the form of South American citizenship, due to which residency agreements are being expanded".
Informed that actions are being promoted to improve "collaboration in the fight against civic insecurity and international organized crime, for better availability of medicines and the cheap internet in all corners of South America and for common and effective measures against the risks of natural catastrophes".
When Europe is in a precipitation, "it does not have an economic consolidation (Latin America) a victorious essence, but it is intended to expand our productivity and show our nations a better future," Humala added.
"We have decided to prioritize a group of 31 symbolic projects that will improve the transport connections of various places of South America, especially rural and border areas..., so that our countries are better intertwined and establish new economic ties," the Peruvian president said.
Referred to the fact that five of these projects concern Peru and transverse axes of its territories from the coast to Brazil, and the other two relate to a better connection with Ecuador, but he did not give any more details.
The closing document also affected the political situation in Paraguay.
"We hope that the electoral process in this country will serve for its return to the Union of South American peoples", from which it is currently ruled out.
Another of the issues highlighted the summit was the need for Latin America to remain a thriving and peace-blooded area that has good neighbourly relations.
In this regard, Columbian President Juan Manuel Santos said ahead of his participation at a regional meeting that he hoped to meet his Nicaraguan counterpart Daniel Ortega in Mexico on Saturday to discuss with ethicipation the Judgment of the International Judicial Court on a dispute over a maritime frontiern that is challenged by Bogota.
"It is possible that I will reunite with President Daniel Ortega tomorrow," Santos said.
"We will discuss all the solutions, none of them are excluded, even the treaty with Nicaragua, which would require negotiations with this country," he stressed.
"I hope that I will be able to tell President Ortego that the negotiations be conducted in the most civilized and considerate possible form," Santos added.
Santos and Ortega will meet on Saturday in Mexico to take part in an inauguration of President Enrique Peñi Nieta.
As part of the summit, EU defence ministers met tentatively to approve an Action Plan for 2013, which seeks to strengthen the dialogue and consensus on regional defense.
UNASUR members include Argentina, Bolivia, Colombia, Ecuador, Peru, Brazil, Uruguay, Venezuela, Chile, Guyana, Suriname and Paraguay, although the last mentioned country is currently ruled out.
Peru is the interim chairman of this regional bloc.
Rafael Correa: South America must learn integration from Europe
Ecuadorian President Rafael Correa announced today that the formation of joint citizenship is a goal in which "South America, in this case really, must learn from Europe".
Correa, who took part in the Sixth Presidential Campaign of the South American Union of Nations (Unasur) in Lima, told TV Perú that Europeans "have killed themselves during World War 2" and in other conflicts, "but now there is practically one homeland."
In this sense, he defended the project for the creation of South American citizenship supported by Unasur member states.
"It is necessary to achieve the free movement of citizens and workers within any South-American country, which is already taking place among the members of the Andean community, but there are still backward areas that want to bring us back to the past," he pointed out.
Among other things, the Ecuadorian president stated that he agreed to reciprocate the U.S. Organization (OAS), on the condition that Anglo-Saxon states' influence is reduced and those who have signed the Human Rights Pact will be taken into account.
"Thousons who are at the forefront never commit themselves to anything, and on the contrary, we South Americans will sign everything.
It is incomprehensible that the Inter-Americean Human Rights Commission is funded by the United States in Washington," WikiLeaks founder Julian Assange said in an allusion allulging political asylum provided by Ecuador.
President Correa confirmed that he did not regret this decision because he did not dissuade his principles and respected his "deep democratic values and human rights".
He added that at the time "there was a reasonable suspicion that Assange could be extradited to a third country regardless of the ongoing process."
In addition, he criticized the Swedish judiciary, who asked him to undergo interrogation on Swedish territory for an alleged sexual offense, while "the Swedish legislature makes it possible to interrogate by video-conferential calls that could take place from an Ecuadorian embassy in London."
Correa said there is a risk that Assange will deteriorate both physical and mental health.
"I haven't talked to him ever since he's at our embassy, but the ambassador informed me that he's had minor lung problems, but nothing serious," she said.
"But there is a risk that his physical and mental state will deteriorate because he is locked up in a small space without the possibility of movement in the fresh air.
This would affect everyone's health," he added.
President Correa noted that a solution to Asssange since June at the Ecuadorian embassy in London under a permit that allows him to travel to Ecuador is in the hands of Great Britain, Sweden and European courts, emphasizing talks with London in order to reconcile imprisoning WikiLeaks.
"We do not negotiate about human rights, we do not use such a word in this case, but there have been constant conversations.
The solution to this problem lies in the hands of Great Britain, Sweden and European courts, because Assange's lawyer Baltazar Garzón is conducting a series of proceedings at various European instances," he said.
He stated that "if the United Kingdom issues a culvert tomorrow, it will all end".
And if Sweden hears Mr. Assange at the Ecuadorian embassy in London or via Skype, as the local legislation allows without a problem and as has already been carried out in other cases, this issue will come to an end tomorrow."
Correa took advantage of this situation to present himself as a defender of press freedom, clarifying that he certainly does not tolerate "under-demeanryness, bad intentions and lies that undermine the freedom of expression."
The greatest enemies of the free press are not us, undermining and perverse politicians, but bad journalists in the service of profit, coercion and extortion," he said.
From this point of view, he expressed joy at the fact that neither such journalists, "neither the bankers nor the hegemonic countries, nor the bourgeoisie, speak of Ecuador", and stated that if he was re-elected, "he will continue the changes led in the right way and in the right direction".
President Correa also backed the decision to further maintain the veto expressed by Paraguay on Unasur's union membership at least until its next election, arguing that the whole "must have to be solid and not to tolerate opportunism and couchism shrouded in legality, because in reality "destroyed the legitimacy of Paraguayan democracy."
In addition, the Ecuadorian president has stated that he considers "quickly appropriate" the wish of his Colombian counterpart Juan Manuel Santos to negotiate with Nicaragua on the nautical border between the two countries now, at the time after the judgment of the International Judgment in The Hague, which ruled Nikaragua in favor of Nicaragua.
The verdict is not currently accepted.
"It's an issue between South American countries.
Conflicencies are unavoidable, but they must be surpassed by the will to walk together.
It is necessary to judge them broadly in order for them to be surpassed and there is an opportunity to move forward.”
He also voiced his hope of settling a dispute over the Maritime border, which he is addressing in the same court of Peru and Chile, expressing the opinion that "it is right for Latin America to seek out international instances if the two countries are committing to accept the verdict, no matter how harsh it is."
In connection with the possibility that he will run in the next presidential election in Ecuador in order to apply for the third term, he noted that he perceives this possibility "with great optimism and joy, even though it is sometimes very difficult".
Correa has stated that if he loses the election in February 2013, he will leave his public life.
“Personally, I’ve never been interested in power, but such an unrighteous situation as socio-economic poverty in Ecuador can only be rectified by political power.
My political movement believed that it was me who guaranteed this probable victor, so we have to accept this responsibility," he said.
"If I had won, it would be my last presidential term and then I'd retire from politics.
When we lose, the same thing awaits me.
I've already made a decision," he said.
Correa also referred to the new health treatment of Venezuelan President Hugo Chávez in Cuba.
