Victims of AIDS today are the result of a late diagnosis
Fabrizi was 21 years old when the result of his test was confirmed: HIV-possitive.
"It's as if lightning strikes me," he says at the memory of the moment of the truth, which the doctor tried to "micidly alleviate", but apparently unsuccess.
The boy hid it from the family.
Decided to take his illness alone on his shoulders and began to inquire about it, with such commitment that he had already celebrated his 43rd birthday.
It is undoubtedly one of the most possing HIV patients at the Civilian Hospital in Guadalajar (HCG), which he came to in 1994 after several struggles with the disease.
Fabrizio has lived with human immunodeficiency (HIV) virus from the age of 22, something unimaginable in the early 90's, when there were a lot of doubts, few treatment options and a bigger stigma.
At that time, even the director of the Clinic of the Mexican Institute of Social Security (IMSS) did not bid fare-by, "because he had a cut.
At that time to have AIDS was synonymous with death.
Now it is possible to survive and fully live with this syndrome.
Nevertheless, there are a lot of people who don’t even know about their disease when the virus caused the destruction of the body and extortivated the immune system and then become victims of arrogant infections.
31 years since AIDS appeared in the world, or at least from the first documented cases, "it marks a great success that the life expectancy of a patient who starts treatment in a timely timely and appropriate manner is exactly the same as for healthy people," said Jaime Andrade Villanueva, Head of the HIV Unit at the Civil Hospital in Guadalajara.
Infectologist and HIV/AIDS psychologist Andrade Villanueva explained that scientists have come to concluding that AIDS has not been a death sentence since 2008, but that survival and full-fledged life depend on the degree of disability of the immune system of patients who have been diagnosed with the disease, and those who don't use drugs are better: people with 200 CD4 cells can live for up to 30 years of age, and people with 500 CD4 cells for up to 50 years.
Simply put, this means that those who are diagnosed with HIV at the age of 25 can, according to these findings, "live without problems up to the age of 75," the doctor explained in an interview.
To accentuate this progress, it is worth remembering that the average life expectancy of Mexicans today is 76 years old.
Although the mortality rate has decreased significantly in recent years and the number of people who have died of AIDS has fallen from 6,678 in 2007 to 4,862 in 2011 (the yearbook of the United Nations Joint HIV/AIDS - UNAIDS program), it is also true that 60 per cent of patients have perished since the discovery of AIDS, according to national statistics.
In Jalisco alone, 255 people have died in 2011 and so far 187 until May this year. However, since 2005, universal access to anti-retroversics has been ensured.
- Why do people continue to die as a result of the disease?
"It seems to me that the problem isn't the possibility of access to treatment.
That's how I see it, that's what's going on in our hospital.
At Least in the last 12 years, we have not exhausted the supply of pharmaceuticals. The problem is that patients come at a very advanced stage, because they do not know the state of the infection, i.e. in the late stage of the disease."
Nine out of ten patients come when an associated infection occurs. The only thing that needs to be done in order to be able to more affect the overall mortality rate is to diagnose the disease earlier, and therefore it is necessary on a large scale to offer tests to identify the disease to all those who apply for them.
His proposal coincides with specialists and officials from the State AIDS Prevention Council in Jalisco, as well as directly with patients such as Fabrizio, who came to get tests for a private laboratory just because his Friend did it, and despite his young age, he has already had AIDS and also suffered from Kaposi sarcom, a cancer that is one of the usual comrades.
"Everything will change when you know you have AIDS.
Some people think they're going to die and they don't want to know anything else.
"If I'm going to die, I'd rather have three times a week," they say, but that's not my case.
The change was for the better, I'm eating healthy, practicing, I'm taking medication."
To this day, his parents only know that he suffered from cancer.
“I live a normal life like everyone else.
“I’m working, I have a lot of activities, I’m traveling, I run an active sex life, but responsibly, I’m paying attention to the other person,” Fabrizio, who has accepted the call to share his experience with the newspaper MILENIO JALISCO to motivate those who are afraid with his testimony.
"Whether they take the tests. If they took the risk, let them know as soon as possible, if they're HIV-possessed, it's better. And if they've already been diagnosed, let them realize that they can live like anyone else when they're responsible."
This message summarizes the motto of the fight against AIDS this year 2012.
Concomods behind the counter
There's a big gap between programs for health and ordinary citizens, said Ricardo Salazar, an guadalayar journalist who finds information about the HIV virus.
The biggest loopholes are in prevention.
In the places devoted to this task "has really increased the distribution of condoms, they used to give us one or two, today they give us packages even a hundred condoms, which is very good, but there continue to be people who don't have access to condoms," he said.
Teenager among the most susceptible to new plagoing infections.
"What do you want that for?" it's a common question full of ironism and judging from social workers, school counselors, pharmacies staff and medical staff that tempt tempted by tempt.
Suggested change this inappropriate dividing. Let condoms not just behind the shelves, but let those hundred be in dispensers in public restrooms, in places where young people often go.
"It's not promiscuity support.
It is not even a matter of "paying beer and a motel," Governor Emilio González reacted when asked if he would have consented to divulging condoms in his county.
It's not such asexuality. It's best to make condoms available to those who are already waging a sex life," he noted.
Key figures in Jalisco
There have been 13,435 cases (12,158 cases of AIDS and 1,317 HIV cases).
This state of affairs in Mexico means a fourth place in new and detected cases of AIDS and 13th place in relation to HIV.
92% of infections occur sexually circulating, 6% blood via 2% perinatally.
Estimates that about 50 thousand people can live with HIV, because for one registered case there are four to five people who do not know about the disease.
