Separate on the case, the court omitted the document sent by the Mexican government as an amicus curiae, in which Vitro’s procedure under the Bankruptcy Act is described in detail, pointing out that society has made it easier by inclumerating agreements signed by both countries in order to confront the law with Chapter 15 of the Insolvency Law of the United States.
In addition, it is pointed out that the country has subordinated itself to the principles of the United Nations Commission for international trade, i.e. rules intended for international insolvency cases that guarantee equality for both borrowers and creditors.
