Héctor "Hetin" Reyes: " Basketball was my life"
Basketball globetrotter Héctor "Hetin" Reyes has been associated with this indoor sport for more than 60 years and has traveled all over the world.
Few people in Puerto Rico have as many remembrances of local basketball as Héctor "Hetin" Reyes.
Reyes was tied before he was tied a stroke to a rollercoaga-season, passionate about the sport for more than 60 years, whether as a player of the younger categories, a BSN national basketball player, the owner and coach of Vaqueros de Bayamón in the national basketball league, or the president of the Puerto Rican Basketball Federation.
"I held a lot of roles in basketball over the course of my life in basketball, including at the same time as when I was the president of the BSN national league in the 90's, the general managing director of the national team and the president of the federation," Reyes recalled during a visit to Primera Hora newspaper reporters at home at Bayamón, where he has lived with Isabel for more than 50 years.
“Basketball was my life.”
Reyes doesn't overthrow.
The wall of his house, which is almost all decorated with photos and souvenir objects from his long career, testify.
Bayamón in the heart
With the greatest turbulence of all, he guards those reminiscent of his tenure in Los Vaqueros from the mid-1950s, where he was a player until 1982, when at the same time he celebrated 15 years as a co-owner or team owner.
"It was my best years, which I enjoyed the most, because I had the opportunity to take part in eight championship championships with los Vaqueros since 1967, whether as an owner, co-owner or a coach.
It was a number of successful years, including five championships held from 1971 to 1975.
And then I bid fare-by with one championship - in 1981 when Jerome Mincy made his BSN debut.
That's when "Cuco" Ortiz, who was an excellent leader," Reyes said.
"I remember that Gene Bartow, who used to be a co-treater here and at that time worked at the University of Alabama in Birmingham, told me: "I've had a two-metre-long player for you.
“Do you want him?”
And that's the becoming start to Minty, one of the best players Puerto Rico has ever had.
Bartow then gave me Raymond Gausse, who became a member of the team and one of our top scorers.
“I remember he’s said that if Mincy brings Bayamun a victory in the championship, Gauss will then contribute to the next one.”
He enjoyed the parchment where he played Gausse for the Los Vaqueros team, he enjoyed, but only at a distance, because in 1988 he already served as the president of the basketball federation.
At that time, he wanted to devote himself fully to his functions and enjoy Minty's placement in the national team.
"I remember how we first defeated the United States in the qualifying Olympic match in Mexico in 1989.
Then came the world championships in 1990, where we finished fourth, but we were supposed to have a bronze if it wasn't for the Canadian referee who let us repeat the final throw-in," Reyes said.
Is the team from the 1990 World Cup the best national team you've ever seen?
It is among the best, as well as the one who defeated the Dream Team at the Olympics in 2004.
But my favorite was the national team in the Pan-American Games of Cuba in 1991, when we won the gold and slapped the U.S. team, which was quite similar to what blooged our bronze at the World Championship.
This team had not only Mincy, Gausse, Ramon Rivas, Fico López and "Piculín" (Ortiz), but there were also teenagers (Javier) "Toñito" Colón and James Carter brothers León (Francisco and Edgar) and Mario "Quijote" Morales, who could not be on the team in 1990 due to a knee injury.
The team, which may not have had a perfect composition, but was able to win a gold medal and bring us great joy, the team from the Olympic qualifier in 1995 in the Argentine Neuquén.
With players on the roster, such as "Canito" Nieves, Pablo Alice and junior Rolando Hourruitinner, who replaced the players expelled because of the scuffle in the Pan-American Games in Mar de Plata, despite all the predictions, we have won gold.
What was Puerto Rico's best player?
Piculín Ortiz was without a doubt.
His figures from international tournaments are immersive.
None of Puerto Rico have achieved such results as Piculín at this level.
Don’t mention his work in various leagues where he played.
Who was the Best Puerto Rican Co-Coach?
That's very hard.
We've had a very good-you-reaching Julio Toro, Flor Meléndez, Carlos Morales, Raymond Dalmau and Armandito Torres.
Of the young, I really like Leo Arillo's work.
Which of your success do you consider to be the greatest in the federation?
That I was part of the most glorious era of the national team from 1988 to 1995 and that at the beginning of the 90's BSN's national league had up to 17 teams in a single-seater.
What didn't you have to prove?
There have been the things that I wanted to implement as a division of the youth categories by region.
For example, the Ponce boys would only play in their area and compete with teams from other corners of the island only in the national playoffs.
Now the kids travel a lot and they play too many matches, which isn't necessary.
I also see a benefit in the mandatory certificates and courses for coaches, assistant referees and referees.
I'm delighted.
What do you deal with now?
Most of all I listen to music, I watch YouTube videos with music from my time, I enjoy my grandchildren and sometimes I go to basketball matches.
And, of course, I'm enjoying the company of my wife Isabel, who's always been by my side.
Actor Larry Hagman
