Larry Hagman, whose role as predatory oil mogul J.R. Ewing in the television series Dallas became a symbol of slaid in the 80's, has died.
He was 81 years old.
Hagman, who returned this year as J.R. in a new series of Dallas, has died according to an outspousic announcement from a family commissioned to The Associated Press by Dallas production company Warner Bros on Friday afternoon for comrades associated with cancer.
"Larry went back to his beloved hometown of Dallas, where he once again played the iconic role that he most liked," his family said.
Larry's family and his closest friends were with him in Dallas for Thanksgiving.
"Linda Gray, who represented his wife in the original and new series, was with Hagman the moment he died in the Dallas hospital," her agent Jeffrey Lane said.
He brought joy to everyone he knew.
"He's been creative, generous, funny, kind and talented, and I'm going to miss him terribly.
He was unique and he lived his life to the fullest," Linda Gray said in announcing.
Hagman was diagnosed with cirrhotic cirrhosis in 1992 and he himself admitted that he drank a lot for years.
In 1995 he found a malignant tumor on his liver, and Hagman underwent a transplant.
Years prior to the broadcast of the Dallas series, Hagman rose to fame on television as a decent guy in the lightweight comedy I Dream of Jeannie aired by NBC from 1965 to 1970.
Here he played the character of Captain Tony Nelson, an astronaut whose life changes when he meets an attrial girl, played by Barbara Eden, and takes her to his house to live together.
He also starred in two comedies circulated only for a short time, to The Good Life (NBC, 1971-72) and Here We Go Again (ABC, 1973).
