Kristin combed with J.R. and he then threatened to call her a prostitute if she didn't leave the city, but there were others who had a reason to attack him.
Hagman played Ewing, an insatiable greed with a charismatic sceptibility: a gunning businessman and an unfaithful husband trying to make his wife Sue Ellen (Linda Gray), who was an alcoholic, locked up in a rehearsal room.
"I know what I'm going to have J.R. on a tombstone," Hagman said in 1988.
It should be there: "Here lies an honorary citizen J.R. Ewing."
It's the only thing that didn't work out for him.
Victoria Principal, who starred in the original franchise, recalled Hagman on Friday as someone who was "great on and off the canvas".
It is memorable and irresistible to the millions of admirers in the world and in the hearts of all of us who have had the happiness of getting to know and loving him.
Ten episodes of the new Dallas franchise were aired by TNT a few months ago with great success.
"It's already been finished filming the five episodes of the second row and the sixth was just spinning," the television station said.
Immmediately after Hangman's death, there was no statement from Warner or TNT about how the series would continue.
Fort Worth in Texas was the son of actress and singer Mary Martin, who shone in classic episodes like the musicals South Pacific and Peter Pan.
Mary Martin was a very young girl when she gave birth in 1931 to give birth to Larry, whose father her husband, lawyer Ben Hagman.
In the early 1950s, Larry attempted his luck on theatre scenes in New York and then from 1952 to 1956 he served with the Air Force in England.
There he got to know the young Swedish designer Maj Axelsson, with whom he got married.
The married couple had two children, Preston and Heidi, and for a long time they lived for a long time in the California city of Malibu, where many celebrities reside.
