A course that explores "the end of the world" begins
Every week, students explore apocalyptic themes such as nuclear war, zombies, viruses and bacteria and global warming.
When Stuart Charmé's religion professor ruled this semester making a course about the end of the world, he knew it was an irresistible enticement: The end of a "Long Count" of the Mayan calendar, which is to come on December 21, many people have grabbed as proof that the end of the world was coming.
Charmé, however, had no idea what awaited him in the next few months: Disastrophical hurricane Sandy, a budget chasm that some people call "debt Armagedddon", and the growing conflict into which Israel is dragged, a place in which, according to Christians theorizing about the end of the world, the Apocalypse begins.
"I didn't realize this was going to be the most apocalyptic semester it's ever been," Charmé told students of Rutgers University of Camden in New Jersey this week.
When one analyzes what is going on in the world today, as if we have 30 days left, it is a really good time.
Respond that bad means good for apocalyptically-minded.
And this isn't the only professor who's offering "the end of the world" this superieuvregation that's theoretically the last one in history.
At the Temple University, Assistant Professor Barry Vacker is organizing "Measures, Culture and the End of the World".
Every week, students explore apocalyptic themes such as nuclear war, zombies, viruses and bacteria and global warming.
"We are discussing why these ideas are expanding with time," he says, offering hypothetical scenarios that govern certain human behavior.
For example, if nuclear material falls into the hands-to-terrorists, a war could break out.
This month, students are analysing films with apocalyptic themes and exploring how they differ from real-life examples.
"I've been trying to invite students what's possible, probable, believable, and the impossible," Vacker says.
On the main campus of the State University of Pennsylvania, Matthew Restall and his collaborator Amara Solari, an art and an anthropology assistant, put together an assistant professor of art history and anthropology to organize a course with the simple title The End of the World.
"We don't connect "2012" because we're always reckoning with the possibility of organizing an odds again in the event that the world doesn't end," Restall said.
Despite "thresponsive destruction", students have to study, create projects, and take final exams.
At Penn State, the final rehearsal will take place on the eve of the Apocalypse, which does not give students any opportunity other than to work "just until the evening of the presumed end of the world," Retall said.
The odds have become quite popular.
"It stretched in two hours," Restall said of his high-diameter student course, which had signed up to 35.
The weeks and weeks before the start of the semester, we received e-mail inquising about whether there was still a place.
Students themselves assure that the course is one of the most interesting.
"I find it fascinating to see what people are doing to comfort themselves," 23-year-old Bridgid Robinson said at Rutgers University in Camden.
Thus, apocalyptic, seminal or religious thinking is only a matter of consolation or its absence.
Will Wekesa, a 25-year-old psychology and nursing student, said he's seen all apocalyptic films.
"I've never heard of an hour where it could be taught before," he said.
"I'm enjoying it."
But there's not an interrogated student, let alone a professor, said they believed the date of December 21.
"Our first project dealt with the Mayan Prediction and we refute it in a certain way," said Julie Zeglen, a 21-year-old last-of-a-half-year-year student at the West Chester Temple University.
"The Mayans have never predicted the end of the world; it's just a key point in the calendar," Restall said.
But he added that there is an apocalyptic annoyance in the Western culture that goes back a few centuries that people are responding to the changes of their surroundings with predictions about the end of the world.
Blossoming of these speculation was caused by the Internet.
"People don't really think about it elsewhere," he said.
This is mostly the case in an English-speaking world.
Joseph Doughérty, a professor of religion at La Salle, who lectures in the Philippines this year, when asked if he knows of a course about "the end of the world", he replied quickly.
"Philippines do not take part in the ends of the world," he wrote, suggesting an occasion at the highest office.
We have a pardon from the pope.
Restall remarked that over the years there had been many days of the Last Judgment, and he said that if nothing would happen on December 21, "people will immediately start thinking about the next date" or philosophizing that December 21 is the beginning of a seven-Year-to-Day period at the end of which the world will permeate.
Students and professors take the date lightly.
A few have said that they intend to go to the festivities of the "end of the world".
"I'll probably call a few friends to laugh together," said Samiro Ford, a 20-year-old communications apprenteator.
